ópolis - TO']\n  tombense: ['Tombense', 'Tombense - MG']\n  treze: ['Treze', 'Treze - PB']\n  tubarao: ['Tubarao', 'Tubarão - SC']\n  tupi: ['Tupi', 'Tupi - MG']\n  uberlandia: ['Uberlandia', 'Uberlândia - MG']\n  urt: ['URT', 'Urt - MG']\n  vasco da gama: ['Vasco da Gama - RJ', 'Vasco da Gama-RJ']\n  vila nova: ['Vila Nova', 'Vila Nova - GO']\n  villa nova: ['Villa Nova', 'Villa Nova - MG']\n  vitoria: ['Vitoria', 'Vitoria-BA', 'Vitória', 'Vitória - BA']\n  vitoria da conquista: ['Vitoria da Conquista', 'Vitória da Conquista - BA']\n  volta redonda: ['Volta Redonda', 'Volta Redonda - RJ']\n  ypiranga: ['Ypiranga', 'Ypiranga - AP', 'Ypiranga - RS']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, re, unicodedata\n\ndef strip_accents(s):\n    return ''.join(c for c in unicodedata.normalize('NFD', s) if unicodedata.category(c) != 'Mn')\n\ndef norm(name):\n    n = strip_accents(name).lower().strip()\n    n = re.sub(r'\\s*-\\s*[a-z]{2}\\s*$', '', n)  # trailing -SP\n    n = re.sub(r'\\s*\\((antigo[^)]*)\\)\\s*', ' ', n)\n    return n.strip()\n\ndef load(path):\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nnames = set()\nfor r in load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv'):\n    names.add(r['home_team'])\nfor r in load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv'):\n    names.add(r['Equipe_mandante'])\nfor r in load('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv'):\n    names.add(r['home_team'])\nfor r in load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv'):\n    names.add(r['home'])\n# group by normalized name to find collisions\nfrom collections import defaultdict\ng = defaultdict(set)\nfor n in names:\n    g[norm(n)].add(n)\ncoll = {k: v for k, v in g.items() if len(v) > 1}\nprint(\"Normalized collisions:\")\nfor k, v in sorted(coll.items()):\n    print(f\"  {k}: {sorted(v)}\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1788201526411,"end":1788201526414}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So there's substantial overlap across sources with slightly different dates or names. The robust design decision:\n\n**Match storage**: keep every match from every source, each tagged with `source` and `competition`. For match-search queries, that's fine — a duplicate match from a second source is not harmful (a search tool returns matches; slight dupes acceptable but ugly). Better: dedupe by (competition, season, home_norm, away_norm, date) across sources, keeping the first (priority-ordered source). For fixtures where the date differs between sources (2490/3040 matched exactly; 550 differ), we'd keep both → duplicates in results.\n\nAlternative: dedupe by (competition, season, home_norm, away_norm) + score + round-ish? Too fuzzy. \n\nHmm, let me think about what matters:\n1. **Search queries** (\"What matches did Palmeiras play in 2023?\"): dupes are cosmetically bad but tolerable; the answer format says \"... (15 more matches in dataset)\" — showing counts. Duplicates would inflate counts. \n2. **Standings** (who won 2019): dupes corrupt the table badly.\n\nBetter approach for standings: use a single primary source per competition-season. I'll implement `primary_source` selection: for each (competition, season), choose the source with the most matches for that season (or a priority order). That guarantees a clean table per season.\n\nFor searches: dedupe globally on (competition, season, date, home_norm, away_norm) — matches that share the exact date+teams+competition+season across files collapse to one (keep first by source priority). For the 550 mismatched-date fixtures between BM/novo... those will appear twice. Hmm, can I do better? \n\nFor BM vs novo specifically, they're the same competition (Brasileirão) and same seasons 2012-2019. The right fix: for Brasileirão seasons 2012-2019, prefer one file entirely. Since both cover the same fixtures, I can drop the overlapping seasons from one source. Priority: Brasileirao_Matches.csv is cleaner (has states, uniform naming). novo has 2003-2011 which BM lacks. So:\n\n- Brasileirão 2003-2011: novo\n- Brasileirão 2012-2022: Brasileirao_Matches\n- Brasileirão 2023: BR-Football (Serie A)\n\nBut wait — BR-Football Serie A 2014-2023 also overlaps 2014-2022 with BM (1126 exact overlaps). BR-Football Serie A per season: 3689/10 ≈ 369/season — that's basically a full season each year. Hmm, so BR-Football Serie A 2014-2022 duplicates BM 2014-2022 too!\n\nActually wait: 3689 Serie A matches across 2014-2023 = 369/season. A full Serie A season is 380 matches. So BR-Football has nearly complete Serie A seasons 2014-2023. And BM has complete Serie A 2012-2022. So for Serie A 2014-2022, two near-complete copies. And for 2023 only BR-Football.\n\nSimilarly Serie B (3677 matches) — only BR-Football has Serie B. Copa do Brasil: Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv (1337, 2012-2021) AND BR-Football \"Copa do Brasil\" (1123, 2014-2023). Overlapping again!\n\nOK here's my clean design:\n\n**Competition-source resolution**: Build a registry of (competition, season) → best source, choosing the source with the most matches for that (competition, season). Then \"canonical\" match set per competition only uses the primary source per season. \n\nCompetitions (canonical names):\n- \"Brasileirão Série A\": from Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022), novo (2003-2019), BR-Football Serie A (2014-2023) → primary per season by coverage.\n- \"Brasileirão Série B\": BR-Football Serie B only.\n- \"Brasileirão Série C\": BR-Football Serie C only.\n- \"Copa do Brasil\": Brazilian_Cup_Matches (2012-2021), BR-Football Copa do Brasil (2014-2023) → primary per season.\n- \"Copa Libertadores\": Libertadores_Matches only.\n\nFor per-season primary source selection: pick source with max match count for that (competition, season). BM has 380/season, novo has 380/season 2012-2019 → tie; tiebreak by priority order (BM first). BR-Football Serie A ~369 < 380 → BM wins 2014-2022; 2023 only BR-Football → wins. Copa do Brasil: Brazilian_Cup ~76/season (1337/10 ≈ 134... let me not guess) vs BR-Football ~112/season. Hmm whichever has more matches per season wins. Both are partial? A Copa do Brasil season has ~80+ matches (including preliminary rounds). 1337/10 = 134 avg for Brazilian_Cup; 1123/10 = 112 avg for BR-Football. Not sure either is complete; take max.\n\nWait, but is deduplication \"hiding data\"? The spec says \"All 6 CSV files are loadable and queryable\". So all files must be loaded and queryable. I'll keep ALL matches from ALL files in the store (each tagged with source file), and expose a `source` filter. Additionally provide `dedupe=True` default for search that removes cross-source duplicates (same competition+season+teams+date... but dates differ sometimes).\n\nHmm, actually simpler and honest: the store keeps everything; searches default to searching ALL sources; dedupe removes exact dupes (same date+teams after normalization). For standings, use primary-source-per-season. That's defensible, keeps all data queryable, avoids corrupt aggregates.\n\nLet me also reconsider: for H2H \"Flamengo vs Fluminense\" in Brasileirão 2012-2019 with both BM and novo loaded (550 date-mismatch fixtures), search results would show some duplicate fixtures with different dates. To avoid that, when both teams + competition + season + round... novo has Rodada and BM has round! So dedupe key could be (competition, season, round, home_norm, away_norm) for league matches. That's robust to date mismatches. For cup matches without round (BR-Football), use (competition, season, date, home_norm, away_norm).\n\nLet me verify rounds align between BM and novo for the overlapping seasons... Actually let me just check: are (season, round, home, away) keys overlapping well?\n\nLet me test this dedup key. Also handle \"round\" formats: BM round is int-ish string; novo Rodada int; BR-Football has no round; Copa do Brasil round \"1\"-\"8\"; Libertadores has stage.\n\nOK let me now also enumerate unique team names across all match files to build the alias table. Let me print all unique names per file (there might be a lot in Copa do Brasil — small clubs). I'll build normalization: strip suffix, strip accents, then alias map for known canonical names. For fuzzy query resolution, I'll implement resolution: exact normalized match → prefix/contains match → alias table.\n\nKey alias groups (Brazilian clubs):\n- atletico-mineiro: \"Atlético Mineiro\", \"Atletico-MG\", \"America...\" no.\n- Let me enumerate.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0591de415001tM3C664LCTw29m","sessionID":"ses_fa6e4b534ffex1G5nGEhqeGwzS","messageID":"msg_0591d4ed8001LLlYw5kWWaPRIm"}}
