th open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nlib = load('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nna = [r for r in lib if r['season']=='NA']\nprint(\"Libertadores NA season rows:\", len(na), na[:2])\nempty_goals = [r for r in lib if r['home_goal'] in ('','NA') or r['away_goal'] in ('','NA')]\nprint(\"Lib empty goals:\", len(empty_goals), empty_goals[:2])\n\nfor path, hg, ag in [('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv','home_goal','away_goal'),\n                     ('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv','home_goal','away_goal'),\n                     ('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','home_goal','away_goal'),\n                     ('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','Gols_mandante','Gols_visitante')]:\n    rows = load(path)\n    bad = [r for r in rows if r[hg] in ('','NA',None) or r[ag] in ('','NA',None)]\n    print(path.split('/')[-1], \"empty goals:\", len(bad))\n\n# Cup round 8 = final? count matches per round per season\ncup = load('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv')\nfrom collections import Counter\nc = Counter((r['season'], r['round']) for r in cup)\nfor s in ['2012','2013','2019','2021']:\n    print(s, {rnd: c[(s,str(rnd))] for rnd in range(1,9) if c[(s,str(rnd))]})\n# what does round 8 look like\nprint(\"round 8 sample:\", [ (r['season'],r['home_team'],r['away_team'],r['home_goal'],r['away_goal']) for r in cup if r['round']=='8'][:8])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788201663400,"end":1788201663403}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive picture. Key design points:\n\n1. **Team normalization**:\n   - Clean: strip \"(antigo ...)\", lowercase, strip accents, strip trailing whitespace.\n   - Extract suffixes: `-SP`, ` SP`, ` - RJ`, `(URU)`, `-EQU`, ` (PAR)` etc. Brazilian states = 2-letter set. Foreign = 3-letter codes (EQU, PAR, URU, PER, VEN...) — note \"PAR\" could be Paraguay or Paraná?? \"Guaraní-PAR\" is Paraguay. In Libertadores context 3-letter = country. Only strip 3-letter suffix in Libertadores file context... Hmm, actually simpler: treat suffix of exactly 2 letters from the BR state set as state; suffix of 3 uppercase letters from a known country-code set (ARG, BOL, CHI, COL, ECU/EQU, PAR, PER, URU, VEN, MEX, BRA) as country (strip, don't keep).\n   - Alias table for full names → (base, state): atletico mineiro→atletico/mg, atletico paranaense→atletico/pr, athletico paranaense→atletico/pr, athletico→atletico/pr, ec atletico? Also \"ec bahia\"→bahia, \"ec vitoria\"→vitoria, \"vitoria ec\"→vitoria, \"fortaleza ec\"→fortaleza, \"fortaleza fc\"→fortaleza, \"sport recife\"→sport, \"america fc minas gerais\"→america/mg, \"ceara sporting club\"→ceara, \"sport club do recife\"→sport, \"botafogo rj\"→botafogo/rj (via suffix parsing: \"Botafogo RJ\" has suffix RJ → base botafogo + state rj — handled by suffix regex, no alias needed!). Same \"Fluminense RJ\", \"Internacional RS\", \"Gremio RS\", \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → vasco da gama/rj... wait base \"vasco da gama\" — is it ambiguous? Only RJ → unambiguous → \"vasco\". Good, suffix regex handles spaced suffixes.\n   - Ambiguity resolution: base → states map built from ALL match data at load; if base maps to >1 state and name has no state → resolve to state with most matches (with an explicit override table for correctness, e.g. atletico mineiro→MG handled by alias). \n   - \"Botafogo SP\" exists (Botafogo-SP from Ribeirão Preto) — so base \"botafogo\" is 3-way ambiguous (RJ/PB/SP): plain \"Botafogo\" → most matches → RJ. \n   - \"Internacional\" plain → RS (most matches vs SC). \"EC Internacional SC\" → internacional-sc. Good.\n   - \"Vitoria ES\" — Esporte Clube Vitória from Espírito Santo?? Actually \"Vitoria ES\" is Rio Branco? Whatever — base \"vitoria\" ambiguous (BA/ES) → plain \"Vitoria\"/\"EC Vitoria\" → most matches → vitoria-ba (BA has way more matches — Serie A club). Good.\n   - \"Fortaleza FC\"/\"Fortaleza EC\" → fortaleza. \"Ceara\" plain → ceara. \n   - \"Santos AP\" (Santos-AP from Amapá) vs \"Santos\" SP → plain → santos-sp.\n   - \"Gremio Novorizontino\" — distinct base (\"gremio novorizontino\"), also \"Novorizontino\" — different keys, but that's OK (they're actually the same club! Grêmio Novorizontino = Novorizontino. I could add alias novorizontino→gremio novorizontino. Minor; add to alias table.)\n   - \"Portuguesa Desportos\" → alias to \"portuguesa\".\n   - \"Vasco\" (Libertadores) → base vasco, unambiguous? \"vasco da gama\" is the full base... plain \"Vasco\" → base \"vasco\" ≠ \"vasco da gama\"! Need alias: vasco→vasco da gama. Then unambiguous → \"vasco da gama\"... but wait BM has \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" → base \"vasco da gama\" unambiguous (only RJ) → canonical \"vasco da gama\". OK so alias vasco → vasco da gama handles Libertadores \"Vasco\". \n   - \"LDU\" → LDU Quito; leave as is.\n   - \"Athletico\" (Libertadores) → alias athletico → atletico/pr → \"atletico-pr\".\n   - \"Red Bull Bragantino\" → base \"red bull bragantino\" unambiguous → \"red bull bragantino\". \"Bragantino\" (novo: \"Bragantino\" SP?) — hmm, Bragantino-SP in BM. Base \"bragantino\" has SP/PA variants (\"Bragantino - PA\" is Remo's rival? Actually Bragantino-PA = Clube do Remo? no... There's \"Bragantino PA\" in BR-Football = Independente? whatever, it's a small PA club). Ambiguous → plain \"Bragantino\" → most matches → SP. And \"Red Bull Bragantino\" is a separate canonical. In reality Red Bull Bragantino (formerly Bragantino) IS the SP club. For demo: alias \"bragantino\" → \"red bull bragantino\"? Careful: novo data 2003-2011 \"Bragantino\" IS the pre-Red Bull Bragantino = same club as Red Bull Bragantino. Hmm, this level of detail is nice for the knowledge-graph story. I'll add alias: base \"bragantino\" with state sp → \"red bull bragantino\". Actually let me not over-engineer; I'll handle it via a small CLUB_ALIASES table mapping normalized display variants → target (base,state). Let me define the mechanism cleanly in code and include a curated table.\n\n2. **Canonical competition names**:\n   - \"Brasileirão Série A\" ← Brasileirao_Matches.csv + novo (2003-2019) + BR-Football \"Serie A\"\n   - \"Brasileirão Série B\" ← BR-Football \"Serie B\"\n   - \"Brasileirão Série C\" ← BR-Football \"Serie C\"\n   - \"Copa do Brasil\" ← Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv + BR-Football \"Copa do Brasil\"\n   - \"Copa Libertadores\" ← Libertadores_Matches.csv\n   \n   For queries, allow fuzzy competition matching (\"brasileirao\", \"serie a\", \"libertadores\", \"cup\", \"copa\").\n   \n   Hmm — but wait: TASK.md says \"By competition (Brasileirão, Copa do Brasil, Libertadores)\". If user asks competition=\"Brasileirão\" should it include Serie B? I'll make competition matching hierarchical: \"brasileirao\" matches all three series; \"serie a\"/\"brasileirao serie a\" matches only Série A. Standings tool: for Série A/B/C separately.\n\n3. **Dedup across sources**: per (competition, season) pick primary source = source with most matches (tie → priority: Brasileirao_Matches > novo > BR-Football > ...). Non-primary matches still loaded and searchable via `source` filter but excluded from default search? Hmm. Let me think about what gives the best answers:\n\n   - \"What matches did Flamengo play in 2019?\" — should return 38 Serie A matches (+ Libertadores etc.), not 76 with dupes.\n   - Standings 2019 — needs exactly 380 matches.\n   - \"Head-to-head Flamengo x Fluminense\" — dupes would double-count W/D/L.\n\n   Decision: build the match store with ALL matches, but compute `is_primary` flags: for each (competition, season), the primary source is the one with the most matches. Default queries search only primary-source matches (dedupe=True default param can include all). This is clean and defensible. Actually simpler to implement: `search_matches(..., all_sources=False)` — default False → primary only.\n\n   Wait, one problem: BR-Football has stats (corners, shots, attacks) that other files lack. A query \"Flamengo 2023 Serie A matches\" would use BR-Football (only source for 2023). For 2014-2022 Serie A, primary = BM (380 matches). BR-Football Serie A 2014-2022 (~369) becomes non-primary but still accessible via source filter. Good.\n\n   For Copa do Brasil: Brazilian_Cup_Matches 2012-2021 vs BR-Football Copa do Brasil 2014-2023. Primary per season = whichever has more matches. 2012-2013 only Brazilian_Cup; 2022-2023 only BR-Football. Note \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\": Brazilian_Cup rounds go 1-8 where 8 = final? 1337 matches/10 seasons. Round numbering: rounds \"1\"-\"8\" — need to figure out which round is the final. With 2-legged finals, round 8 might be final or semifinal. Let me check matches in round 8. Also Libertadores has stage=\"final\" explicitly. \n\n4. **Standings**: computed from primary-source matches for (competition=Brasileirão Série A/B/C, season). Points: 3 per win. Also compute relegation (last 4). Validate against known champions: 2019 Flamengo ✓ (spec says 2019 Flamengo 90 pts).\n\n5. **Players**: FIFA data. Search by name (substring), nationality, club (canonical match!), position, min/max rating, order by rating/potential/age etc. Club matching: map FIFA club name through the same team normalization pipeline so \"Fluminense\" matches match-data team. Position groups: GK/DF/MF/FW for \"forwards from São Paulo\" queries.\n\n6. **Head-to-head**: aggregate wins/draws/losses between two canonical teams across filtered competition/season.\n\n7. **Team stats**: W/D/L, GF/GA, by season/competition/home-away split.\n\n8. **Statistics tool**: avg goals per match, home win rate, biggest wins (by goal margin), for competition/season filters.\n\n9. **Derbies**: curated list of famous derby pairs (canonical keys) with names:\n   - Fla-Flu: Flamengo x Fluminense\n   - Clássico dos Milhões: Flamengo x Vasco\n   - Gre-Nal: Grêmio x Internacional\n   - Choque-Rei: Palmeiras x São Paulo\n   - Majestoso: Corinthians x Palmeiras\n   - Clássico Alvinegro? Bahia x Vitória (Ba-Vi)\n   - Atlético-MG x Cruzeiro (Clássico Mineiro)\n   - Santos x Corinthians (Clássico Alvinegro? \"San-São\"? Actually Santos x São Paulo = \"San-São\"; Corinthians x Santos = \"Clássico Alvinegro\"? Let me use well-known: Corinthians x Santos \"Clássico Alvinegro\"; Santos x São Paulo \"San-São\"; Palmeiras x Corinthians \"Majestoso\"/\"Choque-Rei\" is Palmeiras x São Paulo; Corinthians x Palmeiras = \"Derby Paulista\"/Majestoso. I'll curate: \n     - Fla-Flu (Flamengo x Fluminense)\n     - Clássico dos Milhões (Flamengo x Vasco)\n     - Clássico Vovô (Fluminense x Vasco)? maybe skip\n     - Gre-Nal (Grêmio x Internacional)\n     - Clássico dos Gigantes? skip\n     - Choque-Rei (Palmeiras x São Paulo)\n     - Derby Paulista / Majestoso (Corinthians x Palmeiras)\n     - Clássico Alvinegro Carioca? skip. \n     - San-São (Santos x São Paulo)\n     - Clássico da Saudade? skip\n     - Ba-Vi (Bahia x Vitória)\n     - Clássico Mineiro (Atlético-MG x Cruzeiro)\n     - Atletiba (Athletico-PR x Coritiba)\n     - Clássico-Rei? (Flamengo x Corinthians)? skip\n     - Come-Fogo? no.\n     Keep ~8 well-known ones with names + description.\n\n10. **MCP server**: use `mcp` python SDK (FastMCP from mcp.server.fastmcp or the low-level Server). mcp 2.1.1 installed. I'll use `mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP` — standard. Tools return formatted strings (the answer formats in the spec are human-readable text) — good for LLM consumption. Also maybe return structured dicts? Text is per spec examples.\n\n    Server tools:\n    - `search_matches(team, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=20)` \n    - `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`\n    - `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)` — venue: home/away/all\n    - `standings(season, competition=\"Brasileirão Série A\")` — full table + champion + relegated\n    - `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, order_by=\"overall\", limit=20)`\n    - `club_players(club)` — list players at a club with ratings\n    - `competition_info(competition=None, season=None)` — seasons available, matches count, avg goals\n    - `statistics(competition=None, season=None)` — avg goals/match, home win/draw/away rates\n    - `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`\n    - `derbies(season=None, competition=None)` — list known derby matches\n    - `find_team(name)` — resolve name → canonical + variants found in data + match count\n    - `list_teams(competition=None, season=None)` — teams in a competition\n    \n    That's a solid toolkit covering all spec categories.\n\n11. **Tests**: BDD GWT-style pytest. Structure:\n    ```\n    tests/\n      conftest.py — fixtures: loaded dataset (session scope)\n      test_match_queries.py — Given/When/Then\n      test_team_queries.py\n      test_player_queries.py\n      test_competition_queries.py\n      test_statistics.py\n      test_normalization.py\n      test_mcp_server.py — tool smoke tests (call tools directly through fastmcp's list_tools/call_tool)\n    ```\n    GWT style: use docstrings with Given/When/Then and test names like `test_when_i_search_for_matches_between_flamengo_and_fluminense_then_i_get_matches_with_date_scores_competition`. Maybe add a small GWT helper to make it read BDD-ish. I'll write tests with Gherkin docstrings.\n\n12. **Performance**: load CSVs once at startup (module-level singleton / lazy). Simple lookups <2s, aggregates <5s — trivially met since all in-memory.\n\n13. Also the spec's \"Sample Questions\" table mentions \"Who is Gabriel Barbosa?\" → search FIFA by name — FIFA 19 data may not have Gabigol (he was at Inter Milan in FIFA 19... \"Gabriel Barbosa\" — search shows \"Gabriel Jesus\", \"Gabriel Paulista\"... no \"Gabriel Barbosa\" found in the Gabriels list. Actually the list I printed only had 10; let me check in the implementation tests — search \"Gabriel Barbosa\" returns empty → the tool should respond gracefully with suggestions (closest matches: \"Gabriel Jesus\"...). I'll implement suggestion fallback: if no exact name match, return top-N similar names (difflib).\n\n14. requirements.txt + pyproject.toml for the package. Entry point: `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp.server` runs stdio MCP server.\n\nAlso: \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — that was in prompts.txt sample prompt, NOT in TASK.md. TASK.md is the authority. I'll include reasonable module docstrings.\n\n15. README.md update at the end describing what was done (TASK.md doesn't ask for README update or git push — \"push everything to github\" was only in prompts.txt sample, which says \"ignore this file\". So no git push. But updating README to describe usage is good practice and the README already exists; I'll update it modestly to document the implementation. The task says \"Make sure the code builds and tests pass.\")\n\nNow, some data quirks to handle:\n- Libertadores has season \"NA\" rows (no year) — handle gracefully (season=None).\n- Goals are strings sometimes (\"2\",\"2\" in Libertadores — quoted). Parse ints robustly; empty → None (unplayed match?). Let me check for missing goals: Libertadores \"NA\" season rows may have no goals? Let me check.\n- BR-Football home_goal float \"1.0\" → parse as float then int.\n- novo \"Vencedor\" column: Mandante/Visitante/Empate (can verify our own computation).\n- Date parsing: \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"2023-09-24\", \"29/03/2003\", BR-Football has separate date + time columns.\n- fifa_data.csv has BOM (\\ufeff first column header) — use encoding='utf-8-sig'.\n- Copa do Brasil team names contain \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" — strip parenthetical.\n- Rentistas has trailing space \"Rentistas \" — strip.\n\nLet me check Libertadores NA rows and missing goals across files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_