{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788201794970,"sessionID":"ses_fa6e4b534ffex1G5nGEhqeGwzS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_33431d51a577448d94eea6f7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\ndef load(path):\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\ncup = load('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv')\nbrf = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\nc1 = Counter((r['season']) for r in cup)\nc2 = Counter((r['date'][:4]) for r in brf if r['tournament']=='Copa do Brasil')\nprint(\"Copa do Brasil per season: Brazilian_Cup vs BR-Football\")\nfor y in sorted(set(c1)|set(c2)):\n    print(f\"  {y}: {c1.get(y,0):4d} vs {c2.get(y,0):4d}\")\n\n# BM Serie A 2022 empty goals check + novo season sizes\nbm = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nmiss = Counter(r['season'] for r in bm if r['home_goal']=='' or r['away_goal']=='')\nprint(\"\\nBM empty-goal matches by season:\", dict(miss))\nnv = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nprint(\"novo matches per season:\", dict(Counter(r['Ano'] for r in nv)))\n# Libertadores NA row check + finals listing\nlib = load('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nprint(\"\\nLibertadores finals:\", [(r['season'], r['home_team'], r['away_team'], r['home_goal'], r['away_goal']) for r in lib if r['stage']=='final'])\nEOF","workdir":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-bgij0ztn/retort-d0fbbb4f4ea5"},"output":"Copa do Brasil per season: Brazilian_Cup vs BR-Football\n  2012:  112 vs    0\n  2013:  158 vs    0\n  2014:  159 vs   43\n  2015:  158 vs  139\n  2016:  162 vs  147\n  2017:  120 vs  105\n  2018:  120 vs  111\n  2019:  120 vs  117\n  2020:  120 vs  117\n  2021:  108 vs  111\n  2022:    0 vs  117\n  2023:    0 vs  116\n\nBM empty-goal matches by season: {}\nnovo matches per season: {'2003': 552, '2004': 552, '2005': 462, '2006': 380, '2007': 380, '2008': 380, '2009': 380, '2010': 380, '2011': 380, '2012': 380, '2013': 380, '2014': 380, '2015': 380, '2016': 380, '2017': 380, '2018': 380, '2019': 380}\n\nLibertadores finals: [('2013', 'Olimpia-PAR', 'Atlético-MG', '2', '0'), ('2013', 'Atlético-MG', 'Olimpia-PAR', '2', '0'), ('2014', 'Nacional (PAR)', 'San Lorenzo', '1', '1'), ('2014', 'San Lorenzo', 'Nacional (PAR)', '1', '0'), ('2015', 'Tigres', 'River Plate', '0', '0'), ('2015', 'River Plate', 'Tigres', '3', '0'), ('2016', 'Independiente del Valle', 'Atlético Nacional', '1', '1'), ('2016', 'Atlético Nacional', 'Independiente del Valle', '1', '0'), ('2017', 'Grêmio', 'Lanús', '1', '0'), ('2017', 'Lanús', 'Grêmio', '1', '2'), ('2018', 'Boca Juniors', 'River Plate', '2', '2'), ('2018', 'River Plate', 'Boca Juniors', '3', '1'), ('2019', 'Flamengo', 'River Plate', '2', '1'), ('2020', 'Palmeiras', 'Santos', '1', '0'), ('NA', 'Flamengo', 'Athletico', '-', '-')]\n","metadata":{"output":"Copa do Brasil per season: Brazilian_Cup vs BR-Football\n  2012:  112 vs    0\n  2013:  158 vs    0\n  2014:  159 vs   43\n  2015:  158 vs  139\n  2016:  162 vs  147\n  2017:  120 vs  105\n  2018:  120 vs  111\n  2019:  120 vs  117\n  2020:  120 vs  117\n  2021:  108 vs  111\n  2022:    0 vs  117\n  2023:    0 vs  116\n\nBM empty-goal matches by season: {}\nnovo matches per season: {'2003': 552, '2004': 552, '2005': 462, '2006': 380, '2007': 380, '2008': 380, '2009': 380, '2010': 380, '2011': 380, '2012': 380, '2013': 380, '2014': 380, '2015': 380, '2016': 380, '2017': 380, '2018': 380, '2019': 380}\n\nLibertadores finals: [('2013', 'Olimpia-PAR', 'Atlético-MG', '2', '0'), ('2013', 'Atlético-MG', 'Olimpia-PAR', '2', '0'), ('2014', 'Nacional (PAR)', 'San Lorenzo', '1', '1'), ('2014', 'San Lorenzo', 'Nacional (PAR)', '1', '0'), ('2015', 'Tigres', 'River Plate', '0', '0'), ('2015', 'River Plate', 'Tigres', '3', '0'), ('2016', 'Independiente del Valle', 'Atlético Nacional', '1', '1'), ('2016', 'Atlético Nacional', 'Independiente del Valle', '1', '0'), ('2017', 'Grêmio', 'Lanús', '1', '0'), ('2017', 'Lanús', 'Grêmio', '1', '2'), ('2018', 'Boca Juniors', 'River Plate', '2', '2'), ('2018', 'River Plate', 'Boca Juniors', '3', '1'), ('2019', 'Flamengo', 'River Plate', '2', '1'), ('2020', 'Palmeiras', 'Santos', '1', '0'), ('NA', 'Flamengo', 'Athletico', '-', '-')]\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\ndef load(path):\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\ncup = load('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv')\nbrf = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\nc1 = Counter((r['season']) for r in cup)\nc2 = Counter((r['date'][:4]) for r in brf if r['tournament']=='Copa do Brasil')\nprint(\"Copa do Brasil per season: Brazilian_Cup vs BR-Football\")\nfor y in sorted(set(c1)|set(c2)):\n    print(f\"  {y}: {c1.get(y,0):4d} vs {c2.get(y,0):4d}\")\n\n# BM Serie A 2022 empty goals check + novo season sizes\nbm = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nmiss = Counter(r['season'] for r in bm if r['home_goal']=='' or r['away_goal']=='')\nprint(\"\\nBM empty-goal matches by season:\", dict(miss))\nnv = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nprint(\"novo matches per season:\", dict(Counter(r['Ano'] for r in nv)))\n# Libertadores NA row check + finals listing\nlib = load('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nprint(\"\\nLibertadores finals:\", [(r['season'], r['home_team'], r['away_team'], r['home_goal'], r['away_goal']) for r in lib if r['stage']=='final'])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788201794963,"end":1788201794968}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Key data facts:\n- Libertadores: 1 row with NA date/season (\"Flamengo vs Athletico, final, - goals\") — a scheduled-but-unplayed or placeholder final (this looks like the 2022 final Flamengo x Athletico-PR!). Handle: date None, season None, goals None. I'll parse \"-\" as None and \"NA\" season as None.\n- Brasileirao_Matches has 82 rows with empty goals (2022 season probably — matches not yet played at dataset export; 2022 Serie A was ongoing). Actually 4180 matches = 11 × 380 = full seasons; 82 without goals — probably postponed/rescheduled or the dataset ends early. Handle None goals: exclude from stats but keep in match listings (mark as unplayed).\n- Brazilian_Cup: 16 empty.\n- Round 8 in Copa do Brasil = final (2 matches = two-legged final, e.g., 2013 Atlético-PR x Flamengo, 2015 Santos x Palmeiras ✓ real finals). So \"finals\" = round 8. Also round 7 = semifinal.\n\nNow — check 2022 in Brasileirao_Matches: is the season complete? 380 rows but 82 without goals? Let me quickly verify which seasons have missing goals. Then design is settled; time to write code.\n\nActually, let me just proceed — the loader will handle None goals everywhere. Let me quickly check seasons with empty goals, and also confirm 'Athletico' in Libertadores = Athletico-PR (yes, the 2022 final was Flamengo 1-0 Athletico-PR... the placeholder row has \"-\" goals).\n\nOne more check: FIFA \"Club\" empty for free agents; Position values; and whether fifa has \"Brazil\" nationality (yes, 827). Also FIFA heights like \"5'7\" and weights \"159lbs\" — parse loosely or keep raw strings. I'll keep raw but also try to parse cm/kg? FIFA strings are imperial. Keep raw strings, plus numeric height in inches, weight in lbs. Simple: store raw.\n\nNow let me design the package layout:\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py\n  models.py       — dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamSeasonStats...\n  normalize.py    — TeamNormalizer class: alias tables, suffix stripping, ambiguity resolution, fuzzy resolution\n  loader.py       — Dataset/MatchStore/PlayerStore loading all 6 CSVs\n  competition.py? — merge into loader/queries\n  queries.py      — SoccerQueryEngine with all query methods (pure logic, no MCP dependency)\n  derbies.py      — curated derby definitions (merge into queries? separate small module ok)\n  formatting.py   — human-readable answer formatters per spec answer formats\n  server.py       — FastMCP server wiring tools to engine + formatters; __main__ entry\ntests/\n  conftest.py\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_match_queries.py\n  test_team_queries.py\n  test_player_queries.py\n  test_competition_queries.py\n  test_statistics.py\n  test_derbies.py?\n  test_server.py\nrequirements.txt\npyproject.toml\nREADME.md (update)\n```\n\nLet me now think about the core API of the engine:\n\n```python\nclass Engine:\n    def __init__(self, data_dir): loads everything\n    # match queries\n    def search_matches(self, team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None,\n                       date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, round_=None, limit=50, all_sources=False) -> list[Match]\n    def head_to_head(self, team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None) -> dict + matches\n    # team\n    def team_stats(self, team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all') -> TeamStats\n    def find_team(self, name) -> TeamInfo (canonical, variants, matches count)\n    def list_teams(self, competition=None, season=None) -> list[(canonical, display, matches)]\n    # competition\n    def list_competitions(self) -> list[CompetitionInfo]\n    def standings(self, competition, season) -> list[StandingRow] (+champion, relegated)\n    # players\n    def search_players(self, name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None,\n                       min_overall=None, order_by='overall', limit=50) -> list[Player]\n    # stats\n    def competition_statistics(self, competition=None, season=None) -> dict\n    def biggest_wins(self, competition=None, season=None, limit=10) -> list[Match]\n    def derbies(self, season=None, competition=None, derby=None) -> list[DerbyMatches]\n```\n\nTeam resolution in engine: `_resolve_team(name) -> canonical_key` raising/returning suggestions if ambiguous/not found.\n\nCompetition matching: map user string → competition keys. Canonical competitions:\n- \"Brasileirão Série A\" aliases: brasileirao, serie a, brasileirao serie a, campeonato brasileiro serie a, brasileirão\n- \"Brasileirão Série B\": serie b, ...\n- \"Brasileirão Série C\"\n- \"Copa do Brasil\": copa do brasil, brazilian cup, cup\n- \"Copa Libertadores\": libertadores, copa libertadores, libertadores cup\nIf user says just \"Brasileirão\" — hierarchical: match Serie A + B + C? For search_matches that's fine. For standings require specific series (default Série A). I'll implement competition resolution returning a list of competition names; standings requires exactly one → if ambiguous default to Série A with note.\n\nStandings details: only from primary source matches; exclude None-goal matches. For 2022 Série A with 82 unplayed, standings will show partial table — fine, add note \"X of 380 matches played\". Champion detection: if season incomplete (or even complete), top of table = leader; label \"Champion\" only if season complete? Spec example: \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → standings 2019 shows Flamengo Champion. 2019 has full 380. For incomplete seasons, mark leader without \"Champion\" label... but users may ask \"who won 2020\" — 2020 is complete in BM (through round 38? 2020 season had 38 rounds). Let me not overthink: mark row 1 as \"Champion\" if >=90% of expected matches have results, else \"Leader (season incomplete in data)\". Expected matches per season: compute as max round × 10? Simpler: played/total with goals.\n\nAlso relegation: last 4 by points for Série A (20 teams → 4 relegated). Only mention when 20 teams.\n\nPoints: win=3 (Brazil used 3 points since 1995? Actually since 1995; and 2003+ yes 3pts). For 2003-2019 all 3 pts. Good.\n\nHead-to-head format per spec:\n```\nFlamengo vs Fluminense (Fla-Flu derby):\n- 2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Round 22)\n...\nHead-to-head in dataset: Flamengo 12 wins, Fluminense 8 wins, 7 draws\n```\n\nNow the MCP server formatting module produces these strings.\n\nTime estimate for loading: fifa_data.csv 9MB, ~18k rows with ~100 cols — fine (<1s). BR-Football 10k rows fine.\n\nTeam normalization implementation plan (normalize.py):\n\n```python\nSTATE_CODES = {\"AC\",\"AL\",...,\"TO\"}  # 27\nCOUNTRY_CODES = {\"ARG\",\"BOL\",\"BRA\",\"CHI\",\"COL\",\"EQU\",\"PAR\",\"PER\",\"URU\",\"VEN\",\"MEX\",\"ECU\"}  # as seen + common\n\nCLUB_ALIASES = {\n    # normalized full name (no state) -> (base, state or None)\n    \"atletico mineiro\": (\"atletico\", \"mg\"),\n    \"atletico paranaense\": (\"atletico\", \"pr\"),\n    \"athletico paranaense\": (\"atletico\", \"pr\"),\n    \"athletico\": (\"atletico\", \"pr\"),\n    \"athletic\": (\"atleticoclube\"? no...),  # BR-Football 'Athletic Club MG' — base \"athletic club\" mg\n    \"america fc minas gerais\": (\"america\", \"mg\"),\n    \"ceara sporting club\": (\"ceara\", \"ce\"),\n    \"sport club do recife\": (\"sport\", \"pe\"),\n    \"sport recife\": (\"sport\", \"pe\"),\n    \"ec bahia\": (\"bahia\", \"ba\"),\n    \"bahia ba\": ... suffix handles\n    \"ec vitoria\": (\"vitoria\", \"ba\"),\n    \"vitoria ec\": (\"vitoria\", \"ba\"),\n    \"fortaleza ec\": (\"fortaleza\", \"ce\"),\n    \"fortaleza fc\": (\"fortaleza\", \"ce\"),\n    \"vasco\": (\"vasco da gama\", None),\n    \"portuguesa desportos\": (\"portuguesa\", None),\n    \"gremio novorizontino\": ... base itself unambiguous, fine\n    \"novorizontino\": (\"gremio novorizontino\", None),  # hmm both exist in data? \"Novorizontino - SP\" in cup data and \"Gremio Novorizontino\" in BR-Football. They're the same club (renamed 2019?). Actually Grêmio Novorizontino was called Novorizontino before. Merge via alias.\n    \"red bull bragantino\": keep base\n    \"bragantino\": (\"red bull bragantino\", None),  # merge historical Bragantino into RB Bragantino? Both appear in match data across eras. Merging is the knowledge-graphy thing to do. I'll do it via ALIASES with target \"red bull bragantino\" + state sp... Actually simpler: alias \"bragantino\" -> base \"red bull bragantino\". Then \"Bragantino-SP\"/\"Bragantino - PA\"?? wait — \"Bragantino - PA\" is a DIFFERENT club (Bragantino Clube do Pará)! So I can't blanket-alias base \"bragantino\"! \"Bragantino - PA\" must stay its own team. Ugh. Let me handle: alias maps full normalized names, applied AFTER suffix extraction? If applied before, \"bragantino pa\" ≠ \"bragantino\". Apply aliases to the state-stripped base only when NO state present, or when state matches the alias's expected state. Hmm.\n```\n\nLet me define the pipeline precisely:\n\n```\ndef canonicalize(raw_name) -> CanonicalTeam:\n    cleaned = clean(raw_name)  # strip accents, lower, remove \"(antigo...)\" and other parentheticals, collapse spaces\n    base, state = split_suffix(cleaned)  # try alias table first for full names, then suffix patterns\n    # alias table maps exact cleaned names OR base names -> (new_base, new_state)\n    if base in BASE_ALIASES: base, alias_state = BASE_ALIASES[base]; state = state or alias_state  # e.g. vasco -> vasco da gama\n    if state is None and base in ambiguous_bases: state = default_state[base]  # most matches, computed from data\n    key = f\"{base}-{state}\" if state and base in ambiguous_bases else base\n```\n\nWait — I need to be careful about when state is retained in the key:\n- Retain state iff base is ambiguous (multiple states seen across the whole dataset for that base).\n- \"Bragantino - PA\": base bragantino, state pa. If I alias base \"bragantino\" → \"red bull bragantino\" unconditionally, the PA club becomes \"red bull bragantino\"! Wrong.\n\nSo BASE_ALIASES with state guards: alias applies only if state is None or state == expected. Define:\n\n```python\nCLUB_MERGES = [\n    # (base, allowed_state_or_None, target_base)\n    (\"bragantino\", \"sp\", \"red bull bragantino\"),   # applies when state==sp or None\n    (\"vasco\", None, \"vasco da gama\"),\n    (\"athletico\", None, \"atletico\"),  # then ambiguity resolution gives atletico-pr... \n    ...\n]\n```\n\nHmm wait, \"athletico\" → \"atletico\" then ambiguous base \"atletico\" without state → default resolution → most matches → atletico-mg?! But Libertadores \"Athletico\" means Athletico-PR! Need explicit: \"athletico\" → (\"atletico\", \"pr\").\n\nOK let me define a flat ALIAS table: cleaned_full_name (after suffix split, i.e., base possibly with state applied) → target (base, state):\n\n```python\nTEAM_ALIASES = {\n    # base name (no state) -> (target_base, target_state)\n    \"atletico mineiro\": (\"atletico\", \"mg\"),\n    \"atletico paranaense\": (\"atletico\", \"pr\"),\n    \"athletico paranaense\": (\"atletico\", \"pr\"),\n    \"athletico\": (\"atletico\", \"pr\"),\n    \"cap\"? no...\n    \"america fc minas gerais\": (\"america\", \"mg\"),\n    \"ceara sporting club\": (\"ceara\", \"ce\"),\n    \"sport club do recife\": (\"sport\", \"pe\"),\n    \"sport recife\": (\"sport\", \"pe\"),\n    \"ec bahia\": (\"bahia\", \"ba\"),\n    \"ec vitoria\": (\"vitoria\", \"ba\"),\n    \"vitoria ec\": (\"vitoria\", \"ba\"),\n    \"vitoria es\": ... # no! Vitoria ES with state stays vitoria-es? base vitoria ambiguous (ba/es) → vitoria-es keeps its own key. Good — no alias needed.\n    \"fortaleza ec\": (\"fortaleza\", \"ce\"),\n    \"fortaleza fc\": (\"fortaleza\", \"ce\"),\n    \"vasco\": (\"vasco da gama\", None),\n    \"portuguesa desportos\": (\"portuguesa\", None),\n    \"novorizontino\": (\"gremio novorizontino\", None),  # hmm \"Novorizontino - SP\" appears in cup data... base novorizontino + state sp. If alias applies only to stateless, then \"Novorizontino-SP\" → base novorizontino, state sp, ambiguous? is \"novorizontino\" ambiguous? only SP → key \"novorizontino\". But BR-Football \"Gremio Novorizontino\" → \"gremio novorizontino\". Two keys for same club. To merge, alias must apply with state sp too. I'll make alias rule: applies if state is None or state == \"sp\".\n    \"red bull brasil\": own base, fine.\n    \"gremio\"?? plain \"Gremio\" (BR-Football) vs \"Gremio RS\" → base gremio, ambiguous? gremio appears with rs only (plus \"gremio anapolis\", \"gremio novorizontino\" are separate bases) → unambiguous → \"gremio\". ✓\n    \"internacional\" ambiguous rs/sc → default rs ✓ (no alias needed)\n    \"botafogo\" ambiguous rj/pb/sp → default rj ✓\n    \"santos\" ambiguous sp/ap → default sp ✓\n    \"america\" ambiguous mg/rn → default mg ✓ (most matches)\n    \"flamengo\" rj/pi → rj ✓\n    \"fluminense\" rj/pi → rj ✓\n    \"atletico\" es/pr/go/mg/ba/ac → default mg ✓ (most matches: Atletico-MG long Serie A history)\n    \"santa cruz\" pe/rn/rs → pe ✓\n    \"nautico\" pe/rr → pe ✓\n    \"guarani\" sp/ce → sp ✓\n    \"juventude\" rs/ma → rs ✓\n    \"ceara\" only ce → unambiguous → \"ceara\" ✓\n    \"sport\" only pe → \"sport\" ✓\n    \"bahia\" only ba → \"bahia\" ✓\n    \"vitoria\" ba/es → ba ✓\n    \"internacional sc\"?? \"EC Internacional SC\" — base \"ec internacional\"?? Hmm: \"EC Internacional SC\" → suffix split: does \"SC\" attach to \"internacional\"? Cleaned \"ec internacional sc\" — suffix regex takes trailing \"sc\" → base \"ec internacional\", state sc. That's wrong base (\"ec internacional\" vs \"internacional\"). Need alias \"ec internacional\" → (\"internacional\", \"sc\"). Hmm but wait — \"EC\" prefix. Similarly \"EC Bahia\" → base \"ec bahia\" (no suffix since \"bahia\" isn't a state code... \"EC Bahia\" cleaned = \"ec bahia\" — no trailing state code) → alias to (\"bahia\",\"ba\"). And \"Macae Esporte RJ\" → base \"macae esporte\", state rj — vs \"Macae Esporte FC\" (base \"macae esporte fc\")! Two keys for same club. Alias \"macae esporte fc\" → \"macae esporte\"? Sigh — long tail of small clubs. I'll add the ones I spotted and accept imperfection for tiny clubs (they don't affect sample questions).\n```\n\nI need a systematic cleanup for \"EC\"/\"FC\"/\"Sport Club\" prefixes? No — merging \"Santos FC\" with \"Santos\" needs alias \"santos fc\"→santos. Rather than infinite rules, my curated alias list covers the clubs that matter (Serie A/B regulars + FIFA clubs). For fuzzy user queries, resolution handles the rest.\n\nAdditional aliases to cover BR-Football names vs canonical:\nLet me diff BR-Football team names against my expected canonical bases and write aliases for the notable ones. Names like \"Botafogo RJ\" (suffix handles), \"Sao Paulo\" ✓, \"Athletico Paranaense\" ✓ alias, \"Atletico Mineiro\" ✓ alias, \"EC Bahia\" ✓, \"Fortaleza EC\" ✓, \"Fortaleza FC\" ✓, \"EC Vitoria\" ✓, \"Vitoria EC\" ✓, \"Sport Recife\" ✓, \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → base \"vasco da gama\" state rj → unambiguous? base \"vasco da gama\" only rj → key \"vasco da gama\" ✓. \"Gremio RS\" ✓ unambiguous base. \"Internacional RS\" ✓. \"Cuiaba MT\" → base cuiaba only mt? \"Cuiabá - MT\" also → unambiguous → \"cuiaba\" ✓. \"Avai\" ✓. \"Coritiba PR\" ✓. \"Chapecoense\" ✓. \"Parana\" → base parana only pr → \"parana\" ✓. \"Goias\" ✓. \"Ceara\" ✓. \"Bahia de Feira\" ✓. \"Fluminense De Feira\" — hmm cleaned \"fluminense de feira\" vs \"Fluminense de Feira - BA\" → base \"fluminense de feira\" ✓ same. \"CRB\"? base crb only al → \"crb\" ✓. \"CSA\" ✓. \"Paysandu\" ✓. \"Remo\" ✓. \"Ferroviaria SP\" → \"ferroviaria\" — also \"Ferroviário - CE\" is base \"ferroviario\" different. ✓. \"Nautico RR\" is the RR club; \"Nautico-PE\" pe → base nautico ambiguous → nautico-pe vs nautico-rr distinct keys ✓. \"Central SC\" ✓. \"Tombense\"? ✓. \"Operario PR\"/\"Operario MS\" suffix handles ✓.\n\nFIFA club canonicalization: \"Grêmio\"→gremio ✓, \"Atlético Mineiro\"→alias atletico-mg ✓, \"Cruzeiro\" ✓, \"Fluminense\"→fluminense-rj via ambiguity default ✓, \"Santos\"→santos-sp ✓, \"Internacional\"→internacional-rs ✓, \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" — cleaned: remove parenthetical \"(minas gerais)\"?? My clean() removes parentheticals → \"america fc\" → alias needed \"america fc\" → (\"america\",\"mg\"). Add it. \"Botafogo\"→botafogo-rj ✓, \"Bahia\" ✓, \"Paraná\" ✓, \"Atlético Paranaense\" → alias atletico-pr ✓, \"Vitória\"→vitoria-ba ✓, \"Sport Club do Recife\" → alias ✓, \"Chapecoense\" ✓, \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → alias ✓. \n\nAlso user queries like \"São Paulo FC\", \"SC Corinthians Paulista\", \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" (spec mentions full names!). Add aliases: \"sao paulo fc\" → sao paulo; \"sc corinthians paulista\"/\"sport club corinthians paulista\" → corinthians; \"se palmeiras\"/\"sociedade esportiva palmeiras\" → palmeiras; \"cr flamengo\"/\"clube de regatas do flamengo\" → flamengo-rj; \"gremio fbpa\"? skip. Add the spec-mentioned one + a few famous full names.\n\nNicknames (for demo flair): \"fla\"→flamengo-rj, \"flu\"→fluminense-rj, \"timao\"→corinthians, \"verdao\"→palmeiras, \"galo\"→atletico-mg, \"furacao\"→atletico-pr? \"athletico\" fine, \"leao\"? no (multiple Leões), \"vovo\"→fluminense? keep: fla, flu, mengao→flamengo, timao, verdao, galo, peixe→santos, mip? no. Also \"cruzeiro\"? fine. Keep the table small and well-known.\n\nForeign country suffixes in Libertadores: \"-EQU\", \"(URU)\", \"(PAR)\", \"(PER)\", \"(VEN)\", \"-PAR\", \"-URU\", \"-PER\". So suffix pattern: optional trailing \"-XXX\" or \" (XXX)\" where XXX in COUNTRY_CODES → strip, no state kept. But careful: \"Palmeiras-BRA\"? Not present (only 4 teams found without suffix; but let me also treat \"BRA\" as strippable country code). Also \"Guaraní-PAR\" vs \"Guaraní (PAR)\" → both → base \"guarani\"... wait! Base \"guarani\" then collides with Paraguayan Guaraní only — fine, single club. ✓ And \"Libertad-PAR\"/\"Libertad\" → base libertad ✓. \"Nacional-URU\"/\"Nacional (URU)\" → base \"nacional\" — but there's also \"Nacional (PAR)\" (Paraguay) and \"Nacional AM\" (Brazilian Nacional-AM)! Base \"nacional\" with countries URU/PAR stripped → collision between two different foreign clubs!! Nacional (URU) vs Nacional (PAR) both → \"nacional\". Hmm. To disambiguate foreign clubs, keep country in the key: \"nacional-uru\" vs \"nacional-par\". So: 2-letter BR state suffix → state (kept if ambiguous); 3-letter country suffix → keep as part of key always (lowercased): \"barcelona-equ\"? But plain \"Barcelona\" in Libertadores (without suffix) — is there plain \"Barcelona\"? I saw 'Barcelona-EQU' and 'Delfín-EQU' and 'Delfín' (plain). Ugh: \"Delfín\" and \"Delfín-EQU\" are the same club (Ecuador). If I always keep country suffix, plain \"Delfín\" → \"delfin\" ≠ \"delfin-equ\". \n\nSimplest robust approach: country suffix stripped → base; collisions among foreign clubs with same base distinguished by... they'd share a key. How bad? \"nacional\": Nacional-URU, Nacional-PAR, Nacional-AM(Manaus). If Brazilian \"Nacional AM\" → base nacional state am → nacional-am (state kept? base nacional ambiguous across {uru-country, par-country, am-state}). This is getting too deep. Pragmatic call: foreign teams are background noise for a Brazilian-soccer demo; collisions among \"Nacional (URU)\"/\"Nacional (PAR)\" affect only Libertadores queries about tiny foreign clubs. I'll keep country suffix as part of the canonical key (nacional-uru, barcelona-equ) AND when the base+no-suffix collides with a suffixed version, prefer... hmm.\n\nDecision (keep it simple, document it): \n- 3-letter country suffix: strip from display base but remember country; canonical key = base if base+country is unique else base-country. I'll implement: foreign suffix → key becomes f\"{base}-{country.lower()}\" ONLY if the same base appears with different countries or as a Brazilian state club; else plain base. This auto-resolves: \"delfin\" (only EQU) → \"delfin\"; \"nacional\" (URU, PAR, AM-state) → \"nacional-uru\"/\"nacional-par\"/\"nacional-am\". \n- This requires knowing all (base→countries/states) — same two-pass ambiguity approach, computed at load over all files. \n\nActually wait — simpler unified model: suffix registry per base = set of suffix tokens (states or countries). Canonical key = base if registry size == 1, else base-suffix. And stateless