{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788202243434,"sessionID":"ses_fa6e4b534ffex1G5nGEhqeGwzS","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_9561de65526040a48d6add92","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"Brazilian Soccer MCP server package.\n\nAn MCP (Model Context Protocol) server exposing a knowledge-graph style\ninterface over six Kaggle datasets covering Brazilian soccer: match results\nfor the Brasileirão (2003-2023), Copa do Brasil (2012-2023), Copa\nLibertadores (2013-2022), extended match statistics (2014-2023) and the FIFA\nplayer database.\n\"\"\"\n\n__version__ = \"1.0.0\"\n\nfrom brazilian_soccer_mcp.loader import load_engine\nfrom brazilian_soccer_mcp.queries import QueryEngine\n\n__all__ = [\"QueryEngine\", \"load_engine\", \"__version__\"]\n","filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-bgij0ztn/retort-d0fbbb4f4ea5/brazilian_soccer_mcp/__init__.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-bgij0ztn/retort-d0fbbb4f4ea5/brazilian_soccer_mcp/__init__.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer_mcp/__init__.py","time":{"start":1788202243422,"end":1788202243431}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Excellent verification results:\n\n1. **BRF 2022 Serie A: 379 played vs BM 299 played** → primary source for 2022 = BR-Football (more played matches). My rule \"primary = most played matches, tie → priority\" works.\n2. **Palmeiras 2022 (BRF): 38 matches, 23W 12D 3L, 64GF 27GA** — matches real world (81 pts champion)! Great test assertion.\n3. **Corinthians home 2022 (BRF): 19 matches, 12W 4D 3L, 24GF 11GA** — hmm interesting, close to spec example format (\"Matches: 19, Wins: 11, Draws: 5, Losses: 3\" — spec example was illustrative; real data says 12-4-3).\n4. **2022 BRF top: Palmeiras 81, Internacional 73** ✓ real world; bottom 4: Ceará, Atlético-GO, Avaí, Juventude ✓ real relegated teams 2022!\n5. **Fla-Flu Brasileirão 2003-2023: 42 matches, Flamengo 17, Fluminense 14, 11 draws** (my rough canon kept \"flamengo\" without state since novo/BM-style... wait — in my canon here I split (base,state) but only used base for the FF check — 'flamengo' base works since both sources have base 'flamengo'). With my real normalizer: BM \"Flamengo-RJ\" → flamengo-rj; novo \"Flamengo\" stateless → default rj → flamengo-rj ✓ same key. BRF 2023 \"Flamengo\" → flamengo-rj ✓. So 42 matches in Brasileirão. Plus Libertadores/Copa do Brasil meetings → H2H \"all competitions\" would add more. Test can assert ≥42 and the exact numbers for competition=Brasileirão filter.\n\nNote: my h2h must default to... all competitions? Spec example shows \"(15 more matches in dataset)\" with a total across dataset. I'll do all-competitions by default.\n\nNow, BRF \"atletico goianiense\" — note BRF uses \"Atletico GOianiense\"! My canon here: \"atletico goianiense\" base (no state suffix? \"GOianiense\" isn't \"go\"... the regex `[\\s\\-]+(go)$` doesn't match \"goianiense\" ✓) — so BRF team \"Atletico GOianiense\" → base \"atletico goianiense\" ≠ \"atletico-go\"! Real world: same club (Atlético Goianiense). I need alias \"atletico goianiense\" → (\"atletico\", \"go\"). ✓ (I planned this.) Also \"EC Juventude\" → alias \"ec juventude\" → (\"juventude\", \"rs\"). Let me catch more \"EC X\"/\"X EC\"/\"X FC\"/\"FC X\" prefix-suffix variants systematically: strip leading/trailing \"ec \", \" fc\", \" fc\"? DANGEROUS: \"nautico rr\"? no. Systematic: if base starts with \"ec \" or ends with \" ec\"/\" fc\" → also try stripped variant and alias if the stripped base exists in registry? That's data-driven aliasing — could conflate \"boavista fc\" (Portugal FIFA) with \"boavista\" (Brazil)! Hmm. Only apply the systematic strip for MATCH data (not FIFA clubs)? Even then \"fortaleza fc\" → fortaleza ✓ desired, \"boavista fc\" not in match data ✓, \"macae esporte fc\" → \"macae esporte\" ✓ desired, \"vitoria ec\" → vitoria-ba ✓ desired (but also \"vitoria da conquista\"... no, that's full base). What about \"santos fc\"? not in match data. OK: systematic strip of leading \"ec \" and trailing \" ec\"/\" fc\"/\" sc\"?? \"sc\" trailing: \"gremio sc\"?? no wait — \"SC\" state suffix is handled as state first. If trailing token is \"sc\" it's treated as STATE (Santa Catarina)! \"Figueirense SC\" → figueirense{sc} ✓. So only strip leading \"ec \" and trailing \" ec\"/\" fc\". Plus explicit aliases for odd ones (\"vitoria ec\"→vitoria-ba handled by trailing strip → \"vitoria\" stateless → default ba ✓!).\n\nHmm wait, but the systematic strip must happen AFTER suffix extraction: \"ec vitoria\" → leading \"ec \" strip → \"vitoria\" stateless → default → vitoria-ba ✓. \"vitoria ec\" → no state suffix (\"ec\" not a state) → trailing \" ec\" strip → \"vitoria\" → default → vitoria-ba ✓. \"ec internacional sc\" → suffix \"sc\" → base \"ec internacional\" → leading strip → \"internacional\" + sc → internacional-sc ✓. \n\nBut CAREFUL: \"ec bahia\" → \"bahia\" ✓. \"esporte clube bahia\" → not stripped (full words differ) → keep + alias table. What about plain \"Bahia\" ✓ same key. \n\nAnother collision check: leading \"ec \" strip — \"EC Juazeirense\"? → \"juazeirense\" — and \"Juazeirense - BA\" exists → same ✓ good. \"EC São Luiz\"? wait \"Sao Luiz - RS\" exists; \"EC São Luiz\"? if BRF has it → strip → \"sao luiz\" ✓ merge correct.\n\nAlso trailing \" fc\" strip: \"Fortaleza FC\" → fortaleza ✓. \"Galvez FC\"? fine. \"Santos FC\" → \"santos\" stateless → default sp → santos-sp ✓ (if it ever appears). \"Boavista FC\" (FIFA Portugal) — FIFA clubs go through the SAME normalizer... \"boavista\" base would then match Brazilian Boavista-RJ!! BAD: Portuguese Boavista players would attach to Brazilian club key. Hmm. Guard: apply the systematic FC/EC strip ONLY for match-data names, NOT for FIFA club names (player clubs). For FIFA clubs rely on explicit aliases only (\"america fc minas gerais\" etc.). ✓ Decision: normalizer has `context: \"matches\" | \"clubs\"`.\n\nWait, but actually is that a big deal? \"Boavista FC\" FIFA (Portugal) — players query \"club=Boavista\" → if it maps to Brazilian boavista → wrong players listed. With context separation: FIFA \"Boavista FC\" → base \"boavista fc\" (no alias) → key \"boavista fc\" → distinct ✓. User query \"Boavosta\"... user query \"Boavista\" (stateless, ambiguous? registry \"boavista\" {rj} single → \"boavista\") — user asking about Boavista gets the Brazilian one ✓ and separately \"Boavista FC\" gets Portuguese ✓.\n\nAlso FIFA \"Vitória Guimarães\"/\"Vitória de Setúbal\" → distinct bases ✓. FIFA \"Grêmio\" → \"gremio\" ✓ matches Brazilian. FIFA \"Santos\" → default → santos-sp ✓ (FIFA Santos = Brazilian ✓). FIFA \"Bahia\" → \"bahia\" ✓. FIFA \"Paraná\" → \"parana\" ✓. FIFA \"Ceará Sporting Club\" — leading? no; explicit alias → (\"ceara\",\"ce\") → base ceara{ce} single → \"ceara\" ✓. FIFA \"Sport Club do Recife\" alias → sport ✓.\n\nNow, one more design point: the **registry is built from match data only** (not FIFA), so default-state resolution is driven by match frequency. FIFA stateless ambiguous names resolve via same defaults ✓ (Botafogo→rj, Internacional→rs, Santos→sp, Fluminense→rj, América→mg... FIFA \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" has explicit alias ✓).\n\nAlso \"atletico goianiense\" BRF alias → (\"atletico\",\"go\") ✓ needed. What other BRF odd names? \"Gremio Anapolis\"? distinct club ✓ keep. \"Metropolitano Maringa PR\" → base \"metropolitano maringa\" ✓ fine. \"Duque De Caxias RJ\" ✓. \"Aparecidense GO\" ✓. \"Macae Esporte RJ\" base \"macae esporte\" ✓ (and FIFA \"Macae Esporte FC\"? alias → same ✓). \"Portuguesa Desportos\" → alias → portuguesa ✓. \"Fluminense De Feira\" base \"fluminense de feira\" ✓ matches cup \"Fluminense de Feira - BA\" ✓. \"Bahia de Feira\" ✓. \"Sampaio Correa\" ✓. \"CRB\"? ✓. \"Oeste\" ✓. \"Operario PR\" ✓ operario-pr. \"Boa\" ✓. \"Ferroviaria SP\" → base \"ferroviaria\" — also \"Ferroviario - CE\" base \"ferroviario\" distinct ✓ correct (different clubs).\n\nEnough analysis — the design is solid. Now write the code.\n\nFiles to write (with detailed docstrings, no excessive comments per style rules — but module docstrings are fine and the \"no comments\" rule says avoid comments unless needed; I'll use docstrings which are acceptable):\n\nActually, re-reading style guidance: \"IMPORTANT: DO NOT ADD ***ANY*** COMMENTS unless asked\". I'll use docstrings for modules/functions (they're documentation, standard for public APIs) and skip inline comments.\n\nLet me write:\n\n1. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/__init__.py`\n2. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/normalize.py`\n3. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/models.py`\n4. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/loader.py`\n5. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/queries.py`\n6. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/formatting.py`\n7. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/derbies.py` (small curated data module)\n8. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/server.py`\n9. `brazilian_soccer_mcp/__main__.py`\n10. `tests/conftest.py`, `tests/test_*.py`\n11. `requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`\n12. Update `README.md`\n\nLet me carefully write each file now. Start with normalize.py.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Team name normalization for Brazilian soccer datasets.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport re\nimport unicodedata\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\nBRAZILIAN_STATES = frozenset({\n    \"ac\",\"al\",\"ap\",\"am\",\"ba\",\"ce\",\"df\",\"es\",\"go\",\"ma\",\"mt\",\"ms\",\"mg\",\n    \"pa\",\"pb\",\"pr\",\"pe\",\"pi\",\"rj\",\"rn\",\"rs\",\"ro\",\"rr\",\"sc\",\"sp\",\"se\",\"to\",\n})\n\nFOREIGN_COUNTRY_CODES = frozenset({\n    \"arg\",\"bol\",\"bra\",\"chi\",\"col\",\"ecu\",\"equ\",\"mex\",\"par\",\"per\",\"uru\",\"ven\",\n})\n\nTEAM_ALIASES = {\n    # full-name variants seen in the datasets -> (base, state)\n    \"atletico mineiro\": (\"atletico\", \"mg\"),\n    \"clube atletico mineiro\": (\"atletico\", \"mg\"),\n    \"atletico paranaense\": (\"atletico\", \"pr\"),\n    \"athle