way? redundant; drop.\n    \"ec juventude\"?? — systematic leading \"ec \" strip handles ✓ drop.\n}\n```\n\nWait — systematic strips make some aliases redundant; keep only non-systematic ones. But careful: systematic strip happens only in \"matches\" context. \"macae esporte fc\" is in BRF (matches context) ✓ systematic handles. FIFA aliases must stay explicit.\n\nSystematic strips (matches context only):\n- leading \"ec \", \"clube do \"? no. Just \"ec \".\n- trailing \" ec\", \" fc\".\n- Also full-name forms \"esporte clube X\"/\"associacao X\"? leave.\n\nHmm wait: leading \"ec \" strip — \"EC Bahia\" ✓, \"EC Vitoria\" ✓, \"EC Juventude\" ✓, \"EC Internacional SC\" → strip leading \"ec \" AFTER suffix? Order: extract suffix first (\"sc\" state) → \"ec internacional\" + sc → strip leading → \"internacional\" + sc ✓. But what about names where the whole base IS \"ec ...\" and the strip creates a collision? \"EC Nova Cidade\"? → \"nova cidade\" — if another \"Nova Cidade\" exists... acceptable.\n\nAlso \"sc\" prefix: \"SC Corinthians Paulista\" → alias handles ✓.\n\nNow the **registry** class:\n\n```python\nclass TeamNameRegistry:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._suffix_counts: dict[str, Counter] = defaultdict(Counter)  # base -> Counter[suffix or None]\n        self._display_counts: dict[str, Counter] = defaultdict(Counter)  # key -> Counter[display]\n        self._frozen = False\n\n    def observe(self, raw_name, context=\"matches\"):\n        base, state, country = self._decompose(raw_name, context)\n        suffix = state or country\n        self._suffix_counts[base][suffix] += 1  # None counted\n        return base, suffix\n\n    def canonical_key(self, raw_name, context=\"matches\") -> str:\n        base, suffix = ... decompose ...\n        counts = self._suffix_counts.get(base)\n        if not counts: return base  # unknown base\n        distinct = {s for s in counts if s} \n        if len(distinct) <= 1: return base\n        if suffix is None: suffix = counts.most_common... most frequent non-None\n        return f\"{base}-{suffix}\"\n\n    def display_name(self, key) -> str: most common display for key\n```\n\nWait — there's a chicken-and-egg: display counts per KEY need keys; observe() runs before keys computable... Two passes: pass 1 observe all names (decompose + count suffixes); pass 2 compute keys + display counts. Fine — loader does two passes.\n\nAlso canonical_key for a base never observed (user query \"Barcelona\" for FIFA-only?) — return base. But user queries like \"gremio fbpa\" → unobserved → base returned → no matches → suggestions. OK.\n\nCountry suffix handling: country ≠ state. Key keeps country when ambiguous: distinct suffixes include countries+states mixed: \"nacional\": {\"uru\": 2, \"par\": 2, \"am\": 5} → ambiguous → keys nacional-uru/nacional-par/nacional-am; stateless \"nacional\"... none stateless? plain \"Nacional\" doesn't appear I think. Default most common = \"am\"?? Nacional-AM has more matches than Nacional-URU?? Nacional-URU played Libertadores many seasons (2013-2019?) ~6+ seasons × 6-10 matches = 40-60 matches; Nacional-AM: Serie C/D seasons ~ 15-20 matches. URU probably wins. Whatever the data says.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: for FOREIGN suffix ambiguity I keep country in key — but only when the SAME base has multiple suffixes. \"delfin\" {equ} single → \"delfin\" ✓. \"barcelona\" {equ} → \"barcelona\" ✓. \"libertad\" {par} → \"libertad\" ✓. \"olimpia\" {par} → \"olimpia\" ✓ (Paraguayan Olimpia; no stateless Brazilian Olimpia in data? If there were... fine). \"river plate\" {uru} → \"river plate\" (Argentine plain + Uruguayan -URU collapse — documented limitation).\n\nNow `decompose(raw_name, context)`:\n\n```python\ndef decompose(self, raw, context=\"matches\"):\n    cleaned = clean_name(raw)   # lowercase, strip accents, remove parentheticals, collapse spaces\n    if cleaned in TEAM_ALIASES: return TEAM_ALIASES[cleaned] + (None,)  # (base, state) — country None\n    base, state, country = split_suffix(cleaned)\n    if context == \"matches\":\n        base2 = strip_ec_fc(base)\n        if base2 and base2 != base:\n            if base2 in TEAM_ALIASES: return TEAM_ALIASES[base2] + (None,)\n            base = base2\n        if base in BASE_ALIASES:  # state-guarded merges\n            base, state = apply_base_alias(base, state)\n    else:  # clubs (FIFA)\n        if base in TEAM_ALIASES... hmm FIFA full names like \"atletico mineiro\" — TEAM_ALIASES lookup happens before suffix split anyway.\n        # base aliases for FIFA: e.g. base \"vasco\" → vasco da gama — apply BASE_ALIASES too but NOT systematic strip.\n    return base, state, country\n```\n\nBASE_ALIASES (state-guarded):\n```python\nBASE_ALIASES = [\n    (\"bragantino\", (\"red bull bragantino\", {\"sp\", None})),\n    (\"vasco\", (\"vasco da gama\", {None})),\n    (\"novorizontino\", (\"gremio novorizontino\", {\"sp\", None})),\n]\n```\nWait \"vasco\" with state? \"Vasco-RJ\"? doesn't occur; guard {None, \"rj\"}? If \"Vasco-RJ\" occurred → base vasco state rj → alias guarded on None only would NOT apply → base \"vasco\" {rj} vs \"vasco da gama\" {rj} split. Guard {None,\"rj\"} merges ✓. I'll make guards sets.\n\nHmm, also think about \"atletico\" stateless in NOVO? novo always has state? \"Athletico-PR\" has state ✓. OK.\n\nOne more: \"athletico\" plain (Libertadores) — TEAM_ALIASES[\"athletico\"] → (\"atletico\",\"pr\") ✓ full-name lookup before suffix.\n\nAnd suffix split on \"guarani sp\"?? Wait — is base \"guarani\" ambiguous {sp, ce} → keys guarani-sp, guarani-ce. FIFA \"Santos\"... fine. Cup data \"Guarani - SP\" ✓ guarani-sp. novo \"Guarani\" (2003, the SP club!) stateless → default → sp ✓ (most matches SP ✓).\n\n\"Santos - AP\" → santos-ap (Amapá small club) ✓; stateless \"Santos\" → santos-sp ✓.\n\nEdge: what if base == suffix-only name like \"boa\" (\"Boa - MG\" → boa {mg} → key \"boa\")? ✓.\n\nEdge: \"sport boys\" (Peruvian) — \"boys\"? no split ✓ base \"sport boys\" ✓ distinct from \"sport\" ✓.\n\nEdge: \"ldu\" → base ldu ✓ (3-letter but not country code).\n\nEdge: name that IS a state code alone? None.\n\nEdge: \"paysandu\" ✓. \"remo\" ✓. \"ponte preta\" ✓. \"america rn\" vs \"america mg\" ✓.\n\nEdge: \"independiente del valle\" vs \"independiente\" — distinct bases ✓ (Argentine Independiente plain; Ecuador \"Independiente Del Valle\"). ✓\n\nEdge: \"ind. santa fe\" → cleaned \"ind. santa fe\"? Clean removes dots? I'll also strip periods: \"ind santa fe\" ✓ base fine. \"newells old boys\" (apostrophe stripped) ✓. \"o'higgins\" → apostrophe... strip punctuation except spaces/dashes → \"ohiggins\"? Keep as \"o higgins\"? I'll remove apostrophes/periods entirely: \"ohiggins\", \"newells old boys\" ✓ consistent both sides.\n\nNow **fuzzy resolution for user queries** (`resolve(query) -> Resolution`):\n1. Try full pipeline: alias → decompose → canonical_key. If key in known_keys → resolved.\n2. If not found: try direct canonical_key of cleaned query (stateless unknown → base) → if base in known_keys ✓.\n3. Fuzzy: candidates = known keys + alias names + display names (accent-stripped); score by: exact match > startswith > contains; pick best (tie → most matches). Also try appending each state to bare base? covered by registry default.\n4. If nothing → raise TeamNotFound(query, suggestions=top-5 difflib close matches).\n\nResolution result: canonical key + display name. Ambiguity note: if user gave bare ambiguous base (\"botafogo\") → resolve to default (rj) but include note \"resolved to Botafogo-RJ; other teams: Botafogo-PB, Botafogo-SP\". The spec requires handling variations; fuzzy + notes is great.\n\n`resolve_many`? No.\n\nAlso expose `known_keys` and `variants(key) -> list of raw display names`.\n\n**Competition module** (put in queries.py or loader.py): canonical names + alias matching:\n\n```python\nCOMPETITIONS = {\n \"Brasileirão Série A\": {\"brasileirao\",\"brasileirão\",\"serie a\",\"série a\",\"brasileirao