369 for 2014-2021 ✓ count decides; 2022 BRF 379 > BM 299 ✓ count decides. For Copa: CUP vs BRF counts decide except 2021 (108 vs 111 → BRF by count — but I wanted CUP for rounds!). Priority rule with 0.9 tolerance: primary = highest-priority source whose played-count ≥ 0.9 × max-count. 2021 Copa: CUP 108 ≥ 99.9 ✓ → CUP primary ✓. 2022 Série A: BM 299 vs 0.9×379=341 → 299 < 341 → BM skipped → BRF ✓. 2014-2020 Copa: CUP > BRF anyway ✓. Série B/C: BRF only ✓. Libertadores: LIB only ✓. 2003-2011 Série A: novo only ✓. 2012-2019: BM 380 = novo 380, BM priority ✓. 2020-2021: BM 380 vs BRF ~370 ✓ BM. 2023: BRF only ✓. \n\nPriority order (authority for structure): Brasileirao_Matches > Brazilian_Cup_Matches > novo_campeonato > Libertadores > BR-Football? For Série A 2012-2019 BM vs novo tie → need BM before novo ✓. Order: BM, CUP, NOVO, LIB, BRF — with NOVO above LIB only matters if same (comp,season) — never (different competitions). OK.\n\nSOURCE_LABELS: use filename stems: \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\", \"Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\", \"Libertadores_Matches.csv\", \"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\", \"novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv\", \"fifa_data.csv\".\n\n**Match dataclass** fields (slots): date, time_str, home_display, away_display, home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals, competition, season, round_, stage, venue, source, home_state, away_state?, stats fields (corners/shots/attacks None default), match_id (novo ID). Actually keep home_state/away_state? BM provides states; useful for find_team display. Skip — key contains state when needed. Keep it lean but complete: id, date, time_str, home_display, away_display, home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals, competition, season, round_, stage, venue, source, home_corners, away_corners, home_shots, away_shots, home_attacks, away_attacks.\n\n**Player dataclass**: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club_display, club_key, position, jersey, preferred_foot, value, wage, height, weight, international_reputation, skill (dict of int for the ~30 skill attrs? maybe skip full dict — but \"Player ratings and attributes\" required. I'll store a few key attrs: pace-ish? FIFA 19 attrs: Crossing, Finishing, ..., GKDiving etc. I'll store dict of all numeric skill columns.)\n\nFIFA parsing notes: BOM header (utf-8-sig); ID first col is unnamed index (col \"\\ufeff\"); \"Club\" may be empty (free agents); Overall int; Position like \"RF\"; \"Jersey Number\" may be empty; Value/Wage strings \"€110.5M\"; Height \"5'7\"; Weight \"159lbs\"; Joined \"Jul 1, 2004\"; skill values like \"88+2\"!! (Messi LS \"88+2\") — parse int before \"+\". Special: some rows have NaN → csv gives '' → None. Use csv module (no pandas in venv — pandas not installed; csv it is).\n\nAge int, Nationality str. Keep also \"Preferred Foot\", \"International Reputation\".\n\nPlayer store: list + name index (accent-stripped lowercase) for search; club_key index dict.\n\n**queries.py** — the Engine:\n\n```python\nclass QueryEngine:\n    def __init__(self, matches, players, registry, primary_source, source_counts): ...\n    # resolution helpers\n    def resolve_team(self, name) -> TeamResolution(key, display, note)\n    def _matches(self, competition=None, season=None, all_sources=False, played_only=False, stage=None) -> iterator\n    def search_matches(self, team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, round_=None, venue=None, limit=50, all_sources=False) -> SearchResult(matches, total, team_res...)\n    def head_to_head(self, team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None, limit=15)\n    def team_stats(self, team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all')\n    def standings(self, season, competition=BRASILEIRAO_A)\n    def competition_overview(self, competition=None)\n    def list_teams(self, competition=None, season=None)\n    def find_team(self, name)\n    def search_players(self, name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, order_by='overall', limit=50)\n    def statistics(self, competition=None, season=None)\n    def biggest_wins(self, competition=None, season=None, limit=10)\n    def derbies(self, derby=None, season=None, competition=None)\n```\n\nErrors: define exceptions TeamNotFoundError, AmbiguousCompetitionError? For MCP friendliness, tools catch and format helpful messages. I'll have engine raise ValueError subclasses with `suggestions` attr.\n\nStage mapping to round for Copa do Brasil: final/semifinal/quarterfinal/round-of-16 → compute per season: final = max round that season (but guard: seasons where data truncated? 2021 CUP: rounds 1-4 only! max round 4 ≠ final!). Hmm — 2021 Brazilian_Cup has rounds {1:40, 2:20, 3:32, 4:16} — the dataset cut before knockout finished. \"Final\" for 2021 must come from BRF? BRF has no rounds either. So Copa do Brasil 2021-2023 finals aren't identifiable — honest answer: finals available for 2012-2020 (and 2013-2020 have round 8/7). Mapping rule: final_round(season) = max round IF that season's round count structure \"looks complete\" — hard to know generically. Simpler: stage filter for Copa do Brasil maps to round numbers via known cup structure table:\n- rounds structure 2012: {1..6}, final=6 (2012 cup: 6 final ✓ 2 matches ✓)\n- 2013-2020: final=8, semi=7, quarter=6, r16=5\n- 2021: dataset incomplete → finals unavailable\n- 2022-2023 (BRF primary): no rounds → unavailable\nImplement FINAL_ROUNDS = {\"2012\": \"6\", \"2013\": \"8\", ..., \"2020\": \"8\"} + stage map {\"final\": FINAL, \"semifinal\": \"7\" for 2013+, \"6\" for 2012?...} — 2012 semi = round 5 (4 matches) ✓ quarter = round 4 (8) ✓ r16 = round 3 (16) ✓.\n\nSimplify: STAGE_ROUND_OFFSET from the END: final = last round; semi = last-1; quarter = last-2; r16 = last-3. Where \"last\" = season's max round from a COMPLETENESS table: complete if max_round's match count ≤ 2 AND next-to-last ≤ 4... For 2021: rounds {1:40,2:20,3:32,4:16} max round 4 has 16 matches → not final-like. Rule: final_round = max round r such that count(r) ≤ 2; if none → no final in data. 2012: round 6 count 2 ✓. 2013: round 8 count 2 ✓. 2021: none (round 4 has 16) ✓ honest. Stage→round: final → that round; semifinal → final_round - 1 (count ≤ 4); quarterfinals → final_round - 2; round of 16 → final_round - 3. \n\nFor Libertadores: direct stage field ✓. For Série A/B/C: no stages (round numbers only).\n\n**standings**: uses primary matches only, played only. Teams from those matches; display via registry display names. Return rows + meta (competition, season, played, expected_total = n*(n-1), complete flag, champion display if complete, relegated list if n==20).\n\nAlso validate standings with novo's \"Vencedor\"? No — compute ourselves.\n\n**statistics**: over filtered played matches: count, total goals, avg/match, home wins/draws/away wins + rates, avg home/away goals, biggest margin ref. If no matches → error message.\n\n**biggest_wins**: played matches sorted by (margin desc, total goals desc, date) limit.\n\n**derbies.py** curated:\n\n```python\nDERBIES = [\n    Derby(name=\"Fla-Flu\", description=\"Clássico dos Milhões... no Fla-Flu is 'Clássico dos Milhões'? No: Fla-Flu = ...\", teams=[\"flamengo-rj\", \"fluminense-rj\"]),\n    Derby(\"Clássico dos Milhões\", [\"flamengo-rj\",\"vasco da gama\"]),\n    Derby(\"Gre-Nal\", [\"gremio\",\"internacional-rs\"]),\n    Derby(\"Choque-Rei\", [\"palmeiras\",\"sao paulo\"]),\n    Derby(\"Majestoso (Derby Paulista)\", [\"corinthians\",\"palmeiras\"]),\n    Derby(\"San-São\", [\"santos-sp\",\"sao paulo\"]),\n    Derby(\"Clássico Alvinegro\"? Corinthians x Santos? skip),\n    Derby(\"Clássico Mineiro\", [\"atletico-mg\",\"cruzeiro\"]),\n    Derby(\"Ba-Vi\", [\"bahia\",\"vitoria-ba\"]),\n    Derby(\"Atletiba\", [\"atletico-pr\",\"coritiba\"]),\n    Derby(\"Clássico-Rei (Fla x Corinthians)\"? skip),\n]\n```\nNote \"vasco da gama\" key ✓, \"internacional-rs\" ✓, \"vitoria-ba\" ✓, \"santos-sp\" ✓, \"atletico-pr\" ✓.\n\ndescriptions short.\n\n**formatting.py** — produce the spec-style text blocks. Functions:\n\n```python\nformat_team_matches(query_desc, matches, total, limit, h2h=None)\nformat_match_line(m) -> \"2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Série A, Round 22)\" or \"– vs –\" for unplayed\nformat_h2h(...)\nformat_team_stats(...)\nformat_standings(...)\nformat_players(...)\nformat_statistics(...)\nformat_biggest_wins(...)\nformat_derbies(...)\nformat_find_team(...)\nformat_competitions(...)\n```\n\nMatch line: `{date}: {home} {hg}-{ag} {away} ({competition}{, Round r}{, stage}{, venue})`. Unplayed: `{date}: {home} vs {away} (not played)`.\n\n**server.py**:\n\n```python\ndef build_server(data_dir=None) -> MCPServer:\n    engine = get_engine(data_dir)\n    server = MCPServer(name=\"brazilian-soccer-mcp\", instructions=...)\n    @server.tool() def search_matches(...) -> str: ...\n    ... ~12 tools ...\n    return server\n\ndef main(): server = build_server(); server.run()  # stdio\n```\n\nEach tool: try engine call → format; except (TeamNotFound, ValueError) → friendly message string.\n\nTool list (12):\n1. `list_competitions()` — competitions, seasons, sources, match counts.\n2. `find_team(name)` — resolve variations.\n3. `list_teams(competition=None, season=None)`.\n4. `search_matches(team, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, venue=None, limit=20)`.\n5. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`.\n6. `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=\"all\")`.\n7. `standings(season, competition=\"Brasileirão Série A\")`.\n8. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, order_by=\"overall\", limit=20)`.\n9. `club_players(club)` — wrapper of search_players formatted as roster. (Maybe merge into search_players? Keep both — club_players answers \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" nicely with avg rating.)\n10. `statistics(competition=None, season=None)`.\n11. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`.\n12. `derbies(derby=None, season=None, competition=None, limit=20)`.\n13. `competition_info(competition, season=None)` — detail: teams, rounds, avg goals, final if cup. (Maybe merge into list_competitions + statistics. I'll include it — \"Match schedules and results\" capability.)\n\nHmm — keep tools focused: 12 tools is plenty.\n\nTool param types: season int, dates \"YYYY-MM-DD\" strings, limit int. Docstrings for each tool (they become tool descriptions — the LLM integration).\n\n**Date params**: accept \"YYYY-MM-DD\" or \"DD/MM/YYYY\" (reuse parse_date).\n\nNow **tests** — BDD GWT style. conftest:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef engine(): return get_engine(DATA_DIR)  # cached via get_engine singleton\n```\n\nget_engine caches module-level; data_dir default resolution: env BRAZILIAN_SOCCER_DATA_DIR → walk up from CWD/package to find data/kaggle. For tests run from repo root it finds ./data/kaggle ✓.\n\nTest modules with Gherkin docstrings:\n\n- test_normalization.py: scenario tests for name variants (Palmeiras-SP ≡ Palmeiras ≡ Palmeiras SP; Atletico-MG ≢ Atletico-PR; Atlético Mineiro alias; Athletico (Libertadores) → atletico-pr; Vasco ≡ Vasco da Gama; FIFA América FC (Minas Gerais) ≡ América-MG; Botafogo 3 variants distinct; accents São Paulo; nickname Timão; EC/FC strips; Bragantino→Red Bull Bragantino; suggestions for unknown).\n- test_match_queries.py: fla-flu (42 matches in Brasileirão — assert ≥40 and both directions present); every match has date/competition/home/away; season filter 2023 Palmeiras (BRF primary — ~38? assert all season==2023); date range (2019-09-01..2019-10-01); Libertadores final 2019 Flamengo 2-1 River Plate; Copa do Brasil finals via stage=\"final\" (contains Santos x Palmeiras 2015); unplayed matches excluded from stats but shown in search with None goals (2022 BM rows — but 2022 primary is BRF! search 2022 all_sources=True shows BM unplayed... assert search with team+Cuiaba 2022 all_sources includes a None-goals match).\n- test_team_queries.py: Palmeiras 2022 Série A: 38 matches 23W 12D 3L 64GF 27GA (from BRF primary ✓ verified); Corinthians home 2022: 19/12/4/3 24/11 ✓ verified; team_stats consistency (W+D+L == matches); best home record query? (via stats scan — engine provides team_stats per team; test \"which team has best home record\" style by iterating? That's what the LLM would orchestrate; I'll add engine helper? No — test team_stats for specific teams.) find_team info.\n- test_player_queries.py: Brazilians count 827; top = Neymar 92; Fluminense roster 20 players avg rating; forwards at Santos: position FW group → ST/CF...; Gabriel Barbosa → TeamNotFound-ish suggestions (assert suggestion contains \"Gabriel\"); club key cross-file: players(club=\"Fluminense\") club_key == match team key for Fluminense.\n- test_competition_queries.py: standings 2019 Série A: champion Flamengo 90 pts 28W 6D 4L; Santos 2nd 74; Palmeiras 3rd 74 (21W 11D 6L — verify against my corrected computation! My collapsed run showed palmeiras not in top3 due to atletico bug; real: Palmeiras 3rd. Let me trust spec + verify at test-write time); 20 teams; relegated = Avaí, Chapecoense, CSA, Cruzeiro (bottom 4 by points; order by points: Cruzeiro 36, CSA 32, Chapecoense 32, Avaí 20). Standings 2022: champion Palmeiras 81. Standings incomplete 2023 flagged incomplete. list_competitions shows 5 competitions with seasons. Libertadores info: finals listed.\n- test_statistics.py: avg goals 2019 Série A == 2.305 (assert 2.30 < x < 2.31 or round 2.31); home win rate 2019 ≈ 48.4%; biggest wins sorted desc margins, top has margin ≥5 (Flamengo 6-1 Goiás 2019 in Série A data — assert Flamengo 6-1 present in top-10 for Brasileirão filter); h2h fla-flu: wins sum + draws == played matches; derby name attached.\n- test_server.py: async tests — build server, list tools (12), call search_matches/head_to_head/standings/search_players/find_team/statistics; assert text contains key substrings (\"Flamengo\", \"90\", \"Neymar\"). Async tests need pytest-asyncio? Not installed! Options: install pytest-asyncio, or run via asyncio.run inside sync tests. Simplest: sync test functions calling `asyncio.run(server.call_tool(...))` — no plugin needed. ✓\n- test_performance.py: engine load time (already loaded by fixture... measure via fresh load? A fresh Engine load test with time < 15s (cold load on CI) — I'll time a full load in a subprocess? Simpler: assert search <2s, standings <5s, stats <5s measured with time.perf_counter around engine calls. Plus one cold-load test constructing a new engine from data dir with generous bound (<30s) — loading 24k matches + 18k players with csv module is ~2-4s. Mark slow tests with @pytest.mark.slow? Keep in default run — total suite should still be fast (~10-20s).\n\npyproject.toml: project metadata, deps (mcp>=2.0), pytest config (testpaths=tests), package discovery ( setuptools packages=[\"brazilian_soccer_mcp\"] ).\n\nrequirements.txt: mcp>=2.0.0 + pytest, pytest-cov (test deps — maybe requirements-dev.txt; keep single requirements.txt with all + comment? \"no comments\" rule... requirements.txt with mcp and pytest lines is fine).\n\nAlso note: the venv already has pytest 9.1.1 + pytest-cov + coverage. ✓\n\n**README.md** update: describe implementation, architecture, tools, how to run server, how to run tests, data notes, verification results. (The original README documents data sources — keep that, add sections.)\n\nOne more design detail for `search_matches` ordering: sort by date desc? Spec example lists most recent first (\"- 2023-09-03 ... - 2023-05-28\"). I'll sort date descending (None dates last), then competition. And limit default 20 with \"… (N more matches in dataset)\" suffix ✓ spec format.\n\nH2H counts \"in dataset\" — over the filtered set (default all competitions, primary sources). Verified Fla-Flu: Brasileirão 42 → total incl. Libertadores/Copa? Flamengo-Fluminense met in Libertadores 2019? No... They met in Copa do Brasil? Let me not assert exact total; assert ≥42 and sum consistency. Actually let me compute exact numbers to write a crisp test — the h2h total over all comps with primary sources. I'll compute in the implementation phase and adjust the test with the real number. Better: write tests asserting internal consistency + a few verified anchors (2019 final score, standings numbers, Palmeiras 2022 record, Fla-Flu Brasileirão-only = 42/17/14/11 ✓ verified).\n\nAlso for `search_matches` result \"each match should have date, scores, and competition\" (BDD scenario) — assert all(f.date and f.competition for ...) and scores present for played.\n\nEdge: 2003-2005 seasons had 46+ rounds & 24+ teams (novo: 552 matches 2003 = 24 teams×23×2 = 552 ✓). Standings 2003: 24 teams, expected 552, complete ✓. Champion 2003 = Cruzeiro (real). I can assert Cruzeiro 2003 champion? Let me verify during implementation and add as anchor. (Cruzeiro dominated 2003 with 2003 title ✓ real world — trusted, but verify from data first.)\n\nNow — WRITE THE CODE. Let me write files in dependency order: models → normalize → loader (needs models+normalize) → derbies → queries → formatting → server → __main__ → tests → packaging → README.\n\nActually wait — I should double-check one thing in the FIFA CSV: the unnamed first column header is \"\\ufeff\" (BOM) — with utf-8-sig it becomes empty string \"\". DictReader key \"\" for the index column. Fine.\n\nAlso FIFA \"Club\" empty for ~1.2k free agents — club_key None.\n\nLet me also double check FIFA position values and \"Brazil\" nationality exact string (\"Brazil\" ✓ from Counter).\n\nOne more: FIFA names like \"Neymar Jr\" — search \"Gabriel Barbosa\" → not present; suggestions via difflib on all names.\n\nPlayer search accent-insensitive: precompute name_norm (accent-stripped lower). Nationality: \"Brazil\"/\"Brazilian\" → \"brazil\" match; also \"Argentina\" etc. club param → resolve via registry fuzzy → club_key filter.\n\nPosition groups: GK; DEF = {LB,LWB,RB,RWB,CB,LCB,RCB,SW?}; MID = {CDM,LDM,RDM,CM,LCM,RCM,CAM,LAM,RAM,LM,RM}; FWD = {ST,LS,RS,CF,LF,RF,LW,RW}. Also accept exact codes and words: \"goalkeeper\",\"keeper\"→GK; \"defender\",\"defence\"→DEF; \"midfielder\"→MID; \"forward\",\"striker\",\"attacker\",\"winger\"→FWD.\n\norder_by: overall, potential, age, name. Default overall desc.\n\nNow writing. Long files — let me be careful and complete.\n\nFile 1: models.py","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05928d559001ZTz65j1heztMxp","sessionID":"ses_fa6e4b534ffex1G5nGEhqeGwzS","messageID":"msg_05925f3620018rM50lgnA8nECn"}}
