omp], 2021):\n    src[m.source] += 1\nprint(\"2021 primary sources:\", src)\nprim = e._primary.get((comp, 2021))\nprint(\"primary source for (A,2021):\", prim)\n# count played per source raw\nraw = Counter()\nfor m in e.matches:\n    if m.competition == comp and m.season == 2021 and m.played:\n        raw[m.source] += 1\nprint(\"raw played counts 2021:\", raw)\n# same for Serie B 2021\nrawb = Counter()\nfor m in e.matches:\n    if m.competition == \"Brasileirão Série B\" and m.season == 2021 and m.played:\n        rawb[m.source] += 1\nprint(\"raw played counts Serie B 2021:\", rawb)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788202834751,"end":1788202834789}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The mystery is solved — and it's my mistake in the earlier smoke test, not a data bug!\n\nThe BRF dataset correctly labels the May 17/31 matches as \"Copa do Brasil\" and the league matches as \"Serie A\". So Palmeiras 2023:\n- 2 CdB matches vs Fortaleza (May) + 2 CdB vs São Paulo (July) + 2 CdB vs Tombense (April) = 6 CdB matches\n- Série A league matches = 43 - 6 = 37 (with 1 missing? Palmeiras played 38 rounds; BRF has 37)\n\nBut my earlier smoke test printed `comps: {'Brasileirão Série A'}` — WAIT. That was WRONG?? I printed `{m.competition for m in res.matches}` with limit=100... and it showed only Série A. But now we see CdB matches exist for Palmeiras 2023. Why did the earlier run not include them?!\n\nOH. I see it. In the earlier smoke test, `e.search_matches(team=\"Palmeiras\", season=2023)` — `_resolve_competition(None)` returns ALL_COMPETITIONS — should include CdB... but the CdB matches ARE shown in the full list above? Looking at the first list output again: it shows 2023-05-17 and 2023-05-31 Fortaleza matches AND 2023-04-13/27 Tombense matches AND 2023-07-05 São Paulo. So the list DID include CdB matches. But the `comps` set print showed only {'Brasileirão Série A'}!? \n\nHmm wait, let me re-read the earlier output: `print(\"Palmeiras 2023:\", res.total, \"matches, comps:\", {m.competition for m in res.matches})` — res.matches is capped at limit=20 (default!) — the top 20 by date desc are all December/November/... — all Série A league matches (CdB ended in July). So comps of the top-20 = only Série A ✓ and total=43 ✓ consistent! My bad — no bug at all. The pair analysis (377 matches, 190 pairs, 0 pairs>2) is correct: the 2023 Série A data is a clean double round-robin missing 3 matches. \n\nAnd 2021: 491 matches in Série A with 72 pairs >2 meetings — that's BM 2021 (primary, 380) + ... wait 491 > 380. Primary for 2021 Série A: BM has 380 played; BRF has ~... The 0.9 rule: BM 380 ≥ 0.9×max → BM primary. So primary-only iteration should give 380... but I got 491! Hmm — `_iter_matches` with all_sources=False uses `_is_primary` which checks `self._primary.get((competition, season))` — for (A, 2021): BM 380 vs BRF? If BRF 2021 Serie A played count is high... 491-380 = 111 extra. Wait — `_is_primary` returns True if source == primary OR primary is None. Hmm, 491 = 380 (BM) + 111 (BRF)? That means BRF matches are ALSO primary?? Unless... OH! I bet the bug: `load_data` computes primary from `matches` — fine. But `_compute_primary_sources` counts only PLAYED matches. For (A, 2021): BM played 380, BRF played N. Primary = BM (first in priority meeting threshold). So _is_primary(BRF match) = False... unless the key lookup differs! `self._primary.get((match.competition, match.season))` — competition \"Brasileirão Série A\", season int 2021 ✓...\n\nHmm wait — but the check I ran: `e._iter_matches([comp], season)` — with all_sources=False → filters primary. Got 491?? Let me recheck — maybe the issue is my test called with season as INT 2021 but... let me debug directly. Actually — WAIT. I passed `season` as int in the loop `for season in (2023, 2022, 2021)`. In `_iter_matches`, `if season is not None and match.season != season: continue` ✓ int comparison. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at _is_primary again:\n\n```python\ndef _is_primary(self, match: Match) -> bool:\n    source = self._primary.get((match.competition, match.season))\n    return source is None or match.source == source\n```\n\nFor a BRF 2021 Serie A match: primary[(A,2021)] = \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\" presumably → BRF match → False → excluded. So how 491?!\n\nUnless primary[(A,2021)] is NOT BM... `_compute_primary_sources`: counts from PLAYED matches: BM 2021 = 380 played? Let me check BM 2021: BM has 380 rows/season; 2021 all played? 2021 season completed ✓ 380 played. BRF 2021 Serie A: maybe 380 played too. best = 380. Loop over SOURCE_PRIORITY: BM first: count 380 ≥ 380×0.9 ✓ → primary = BM. Then _is_primary excludes BRF. 491 impossible... unless BM 2021 has fewer played (some NA) making best=380 from BRF and BM=298?? For 2021?? No — 2021 season was complete before dataset export.\n\nHmm, wait — actually let me recheck: is it 2021-specific? \"Brasileirão Série A 2021: 491 matches, 229 pairs\" — 229 pairs ≈ C(22,2)=231. 229 pairs with 72 pairs meeting >2. 491 = 380 + 111. BRF Serie A 2021 = ~369 played... 380+369=749 ≠ 491. BM 380 + BRF 111?? Odd number.\n\nHmm, OR: 491 could include BM 2021 (380) + NOVO 2021 (380)?? No, that's 760.\n\nWait, maybe my pair-analysis loop bug: `e._iter_matches([comp], season)` — I passed competitions=[comp] where comp = \"Brasileirão Série A\" — clean. Hmm, let me just rerun the 2021 case and inspect sources of the 491.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05931d6ef001lXLjdRtArs5UB7","sessionID":"ses_fa6e4b534ffex1G5nGEhqeGwzS","messageID":"msg_059315984001gxPZTUeIAI0PIU"}}
