378 played, 22 teams, expected 462\nBrasileirão Série B 2020: 302 played, 20 teams, expected 380\nBrasileirão Série B 2021: 446 played, 28 teams, expected 756\nBrasileirão Série B 2022: 380 played, 22 teams, expected 462\nBrasileirão Série B 2023: 380 played, 20 teams, expected 380\nBrasileirão Série C 2014: 81 played, 20 teams, expected 380\nBrasileirão Série C 2015: 157 played, 23 teams, expected 506\nBrasileirão Série C 2016: 181 played, 21 teams, expected 420\nBrasileirão Série C 2017: 179 played, 24 teams, expected 552\nBrasileirão Série C 2018: 192 played, 22 teams, expected 462\nBrasileirão Série C 2019: 194 played, 20 teams, expected 380\nBrasileirão Série C 2020: 179 played, 20 teams, expected 380\nBrasileirão Série C 2021: 216 played, 24 teams, expected 552\nBrasileirão Série C 2022: 213 played, 20 teams, expected 380\nBrasileirão Série C 2023: 215 played, 20 teams, expected 380\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nfrom brazilian_soccer_mcp.queries import get_engine\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\ne = get_engine(\"data/kaggle\")\n\n# 2021 Serie A BRF pairs > 2\nbrf21 = [m for m in e.matches if m.source==\"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\" and m.season==2021 and m.competition==\"Brasileirão Série A\"]\npairs = defaultdict(list)\nfor m in brf21:\n    pairs[frozenset((m.home_key, m.away_key))].append(m)\nextra = {p: ms for p, ms in pairs.items() if len(ms) > 2}\nprint(\"Serie A 2021 BRF pairs>2:\", len(extra))\nfor p, ms in list(extra.items())[:6]:\n    print(\" \", sorted(p))\n    for m in sorted(ms, key=lambda x: x.date):\n        print(\"    \", m.date, m.home_display, m.home_goals, \"-\", m.away_goals, m.away_display)\n\n# check if same fixtures exist under other competition labels in BRF 2021\nby_sig = defaultdict(list)\nfor m in e.matches:\n    if m.source==\"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\" and m.season==2021:\n        by_sig[(m.date, m.home_key, m.away_key)].append(m.competition)\ncross = {k: v for k, v in by_sig.items() if len(set(v)) > 1}\nprint(\"\\nfixtures with multiple competition labels in BRF 2021:\", len(cross))\nfor k, v in list(cross.items())[:8]:\n    print(\"   \", k, v)\n\n# how inflated are Serie B/C across seasons?\nfor comp in (\"Brasileirão Série B\", \"Brasileirão Série C\"):\n    for season in range(2014, 2024):\n        ms = [m for m in e.matches if m.competition==comp and m.season==season and m.played]\n        if not ms: continue\n        teams = {m.home_key for m in ms} | {m.away_key for m in ms}\n        expected = len(teams)*(len(teams)-1)\n        flag = \" <-- INFLATED\" if len(ms) > expected * 1.05 else \"\"\n        print(f\"{comp} {season}: {len(ms)} played, {len(teams)} teams, expected {expected}{flag}\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1788202873002,"end":1788202873039}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now it's clear! **BR-Football \"Serie A\" 2021 contains 491 played matches** — way more than the real 380. The BRF dataset's 2021 \"Serie A\" includes mislabeled Copa do Brasil 2021 matches (and maybe more). And since 491 > 380, my \"most played matches wins\" rule picked BRF as primary for 2021 — CORRUPTING the 2021 standings!\n\nAlso Serie B 2021: BRF has 446 > 380 (real Serie B = 380) — mislabeled matches inflate it too (Serie B is BRF-only, so no primary competition — the mislabeled matches pollute it regardless).\n\nSo the BRF dataset (at least for 2021) mixes cup matches into league labels. My primary-source rule \"max played count\" is vulnerable to inflated counts. Better rule: for league competitions, prefer the source whose match count is closest to a valid round-robin structure (or simply prefer SOURCE_PRIORITY whenever the higher-priority source has ≥90% of the STRUCTURAL expected count, not of the max).\n\nCleaner approach: expected count for a league season = 380 (20 teams) — but team count varies (24 in 2003, 22 in 2004-2005...). Structural validation: number of distinct teams n → expected n(n-1). A \"healthy\" source count is ≤ expected and pairs ≤2. Rather than deep validation, use a smarter primary rule:\n\n**Primary rule v2**: For each (competition, season):\n1. Compute for each source: played count, distinct pair count, teams count.\n2. Compute structural max = teams×(teams-1) using the MINIMUM teams count among sources (or from the highest-priority source).\n3. Prefer sources in priority order whose played count ≤ structural_max × 1.05 (i.e., not inflated).\n\nHmm, but for 2021: BM teams=20 → structural 380. BRF played=491 > 380×1.05 → BRF disqualified (inflated). BM 380 ≤ 399 ✓ → BM primary ✓. For 2022: BM teams=20 → structural 380; BM played 299 ✓; BRF played 379 ✓ (≤399) — both valid → priority picks BM?? But earlier I wanted BRF for 2022 (more played)! 2022 BM has 299/380 played (dataset exported before postponed matches were replayed) — BRF 379 more complete. With rule v2, priority picks BM (299 played, valid) → 2022 standings less complete (Palmeiras still champion-ish). Hmm.\n\nRefine: among \"structurally valid\" sources (played ≤ teams×(teams-1)×1.05 and pairs ≤ 2 — I can also validate pairs), pick the one with MOST played matches; tie → priority. For 2021: valid = BM(380) [BRF invalid 491] → BM ✓. For 2022: valid = BM(299), BRF(379) → BRF (most) ✓. For 2012-2019: BM(380) vs novo(380) tie → BM ✓ (priority). For 2023: BRF only (377, teams 20 ✓ valid) ✓. Serie B 2021: BRF only — 446 > 380?? teams=20 → structural 380; 446 > 399 → INVALID — but it's the only source! When all sources invalid → use priority order as-is (can't do better) but the pair-level dedup could still help. Serie B 2021 BRF: 446 matches, 235 pairs, 22 pairs >2. Since BRF is the only source, standings for Série B 2021 would be corrupted by 66 extra matches.\n\nDeeper fix: **pair-level structural dedup for league competitions**: for Série A/B/C, per season, drop matches beyond 2 per unordered pair. Which 2 to keep when a pair meets 3+ times? E.g. pair (spfc, palmeiras) 2021 \"Serie A\": 2 real league + 1-2 CdB mislabeled. Keeping the wrong 2 corrupts W/D/L. How to choose? Heuristic: keep the FIRST two chronologically per venue pattern? A double round-robin has exactly one home leg each. For a pair meeting 3×: keep one match per venue... if 2 home + 1 away → keep first home + first away? The CdB legs could be same venue as a league leg... \n\nHmm, honestly — how deep do I want to go? The spec asks: standings by season \"calculated from match results\". Data quality notes in the spec acknowledge inconsistencies but not mislabels. The pragmatic sweet spot:\n\n1. Primary-source rule v2 (validity = not inflated beyond structure) — fixes Série A 2021 (BM primary, clean 380).\n2. Pair-level dedup within the primary source for league competitions: when a pair meets >2 times, keep at most 2 — selection heuristic: prefer distinct venues, earliest legs. Apply ONLY when needed (Série B 2021 etc.). Document as data-quality handling.\n3. Standings also validate: if played > expected → note \"source data contains X extra matches beyond a standard double round-robin; extra fixtures dropped\".\n\nLet me check what Série B 2021 pairs>2 look like — are they plausibly CdB mislabels? And check other seasons' Série B/C counts for inflation. Also — does the CdB-labeled BRF data ALSO contain the correctly-labeled versions of these matches (dupes across tournament labels)? For Palmeiras-Fortaleza 2023, the May matches were labeled CdB (correct) and NOT in Serie A. For 2021 pairs>2, maybe the same match appears both as \"Serie A\" and \"Copa do Brasil\"? If the correctly-labeled CdB copy exists, I can detect exact dupes (same date/teams/score across different tournament labels) and drop the mislabeled one!\n\nLet me analyze 2021: check pairs meeting >2 in \"Serie A\" BRF 2021, and whether identical matches exist under other tournament labels.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059326c45001rxwTiSs3r1kYf3","sessionID":"ses_fa6e4b534ffex1G5nGEhqeGwzS","messageID":"msg_05931dce3001gmsD0RCwFe7M82"}}
