t":"Now I understand the BRF 2021 anomaly: the extra \"Serie A\" 2021 matches are all dated **2021-01-xx, 2021-02-xx and 2021-12-xx** — these are the 2020 Copa do Brasil knockout matches (played Dec 2020–Feb 2021 due to COVID) and 2021 CdB early rounds (played Dec 2021)! E.g., \"2021-01-31 Internacional 2-1 Bragantino\" = Copa do Brasil 2020 semifinal leg; \"2021-12-10 Bragantino 1-0 Internacional\" = CdB 2021. They're labeled \"Serie A\" in BRF because the date year is 2021 and the scraper mislabeled them. So BRF 2021 \"Serie A\" = 380 real league + 111 mislabeled CdB matches.\n\nInteresting: Série B/C are NOT inflated beyond expectations except 2021 Série B (446 played, 28 teams — includes CdB mislabels + probably Série B is otherwise partial: many seasons are partial like 2014 (275/380), 2017 (381/506 with 23 teams — real Serie B 2017 had 20 teams → 381 matches with 23 \"teams\" means some CdB teams leaked in too).\n\nSérie C seasons are all partial (~180-215 matches) — real Série C has a different format (first a group phase then knockout) so partial data is expected.\n\nOK here's my decision:\n1. **Primary-source rule v2**: for LEAGUE competitions (Série A/B/C), a source is \"structurally valid\" if its played count ≤ teams×(teams-1) computed from that source's own team count — no wait, that's always true by construction (teams derived from matches). Validity must use pair counts: valid if NO pair meets more than 2 times... For BRF 2021 Serie A: 72 pairs >2 → INVALID. BM 2021: 0 pairs >2 → VALID. Primary = valid source with most played (tie → priority).\n   \n   For Série B 2021 (BRF only, invalid): no valid source → use the invalid one but apply pair-level trimming (see 2).\n   \n   For Série B 2017 (381 played, 23 teams, pairs?): let me check pairs>2 there too. If pairs ≤2 → the 23 \"teams\" include promoted/relegated... no wait, 23 distinct teams in one season's matches can't happen in a 20-team league with pairs≤2. Hmm — 22 pairs... whatever: the pair-validity check catches real corruption; odd team counts just reflect partial data.\n\n2. **Pair-level trimming for league competitions** (applied to primary matches in standings AND to search defaults? Hmm): for each season, per unordered pair, keep at most 2 matches. Selection: sort the pair's matches by date; keep matches greedily ensuring ≤1 per... no — real double round-robin = 2 legs, one per venue. Selection heuristic: prefer the 2 matches with distinct venues (one home each side); among same-venue candidates prefer... the LAST two? For the mislabeled CdB matches: e.g. Internacional-Bragantino: legs 2021-01-31 (CdB2020 semi, Inter home), 2021-10-22 (league, Inter home), 2021-12-10 (CdB2021, Bragantino home). Real league legs: hmm — the real 2021 league Internacional x Bragantino: Bragantino 1-1 Inter (Oct?) and Inter 3-1 Bragantino... The data has Inter 2-1 (Jan, CdB), Inter 1-1 (Oct, league), Bragantino 1-0 (Dec, CdB). The league legs are actually... ugh, hard to know which are league.\n\n   This is getting too deep for a demo server. SIMPLER, more defensible approach: **standings use pair-trimmed matches with venue-balanced selection**, and I document it. Actually you know what — let me simplify decisively:\n\n   **For standings only**: build the table from primary matches, but per pair keep at most 2 matches, preferring venue-balanced, earliest-first... \n\n   Hmm wait, actually — for Série A 2021 primary will be BM (valid) after rule v2, so trimming won't trigger for Série A at all (BM/novo are clean; BRF only primary for 2023 (clean, 0 pairs>2) and 2022 (379, clean)). The only corrupted-standings case is Série B 2021 (BRF-only, 22 pairs>2) and maybe Série B 2017 (23 teams). For those, trimming with a heuristic is better than raw corruption.\n\n   Let me check Série B 2017 pairs quickly in the fix. And Série B 2021: 446 played, 28 teams, 22 pairs >2.\n\n   Decision: implement `_league_primary_matches(competition, season)` used by standings/team-stats/statistics/search (the default primary iteration): \n   - select primary source via rule v2\n   - if league competition and any pair >2: trim per pair to 2, preferring venue balance then earliest dates. \n   \n   Wait — should trimming apply to search too? If a user searches \"Internacional 2021 matches\", with BRF non-primary they get BM's 38 anyway. For Série B 2021 search — trimmed = fewer dupes shown ✓ better.\n\n   Hmm, but careful — trimming drops matches that ARE in the source data. For search results, showing 3 meetings between two teams in \"Série B 2021\" (because the source mislabeled) vs hiding one... I think trimming is the right default (it's what \"the data says about Série B 2021\"), with all_sources=True available for raw access. And for Copa do Brasil / Libertadores (knockout cups), pairs CAN legitimately meet 3+ times (two legs per round + earlier rounds!) — trimming applies ONLY to league competitions. ✓\n\n3. Also apply rule v2's validity to **Copa do Brasil** primary selection? Cup structure varies; can't structurally validate. Keep \"most played wins\" with 0.9 priority tolerance for cups. For CdB 2021: CUP 108 vs BRF 111 → CUP ≥ 99.9 ✓ priority CUP primary ✓ (CUP has proper rounds).\n\nNow implement:\n\n```python\ndef _compute_primary_sources(matches):\n    LEAGUES = {A, B, C}\n    counts, pairs_by_source = defaultdict(Counter), defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(Counter))\n    for m in matches if played:\n        counts[key][m.source] += 1\n        if m.competition in LEAGUES:\n            pairs[key][m.source][frozenset((m.home_key, m.away_key))] += 1\n    for key in counts:\n        sources = counts[key]\n        best = max(sources.values())\n        # structurally valid sources: league pair counts <= 2\n        def valid(s):\n            if competition in LEAGUES:\n                return all(c <= 2 for c in pairs[key][s].values())\n            return True\n        candidates = [s for s in sources if valid(s) and counts[s] >= best*0.9]\n        if not candidates: candidates = list(sources)  # fall back to all\n        # pick by most played, tie by priority\n        primary = max(candidates, key=lambda s: (counts[s], -priority_index(s)))\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm wait — for 2021 Série A: BM valid (380), BRF invalid (491). best=491. candidates = [BM] (380 ≥ 441.9? NO! 380 < 491×0.9=441.9!). Dang — the 0.9-of-max tolerance kicks BM out! I need: candidates = valid sources; if any valid → pick among valid by (count, priority). If none valid → pick by (count, priority) among all. Drop the 0.9 tolerance when validity distinguishes:\n\n```python\nvalid_sources = [s for s in sources if valid(s)]\nif valid_sources:\n    pool = valid_sources\nelse:\n    pool = list(sources)\n# within pool: most played; tie → priority\nprimary = max(pool, key=lambda s: (counts[s], -priority_idx(s)))\n```\n\nCheck all cases:\n- A 2012-2019: BM 380 valid, novo 380 valid → tie count → priority BM ✓\n- A 2020: BM 380 valid; BRF ~370 valid (pairs ≤2? need check) → BM wins count ✓\n- A 2021: BM 380 valid; BRF invalid → BM ✓✓ (fixed!)\n- A 2022: BM 299 valid; BRF 379 valid → BRF most ✓\n- A 2023: BRF 377 valid (pairs 0>2 ✓) only ✓\n- A 2003-2011: novo only ✓\n- B: BRF only every season; valid where pairs≤2; 2021 B (446, pairs>2) → invalid pool → BRF anyway + trimming in standings ✓\n- CdB: CUP vs BRF — no validity check (cup) → most played, but I ALSO want the priority-tolerance for CUP 2021 (108 vs 111 → want CUP for rounds)... \"most played wins\" gives BRF 2021. Hmm — my original 0.9-tolerance existed exactly for that. Let me keep a light version: for cups (no validity), use the tolerance rule: highest-priority source with count ≥ 0.9×best.\n\nUnified algorithm:\n```python\nif competition in LEAGUES:\n    valid = [s for s in sources if pair-structure valid]\n    pool = valid or list(sources)\n    primary = max(pool, key=(count, -prio))\nelse:\n    for s in PRIORITY: if counts[s] >= best*0.9: primary = s; break\n    else: primary = most_common\n```\n\nWait — one more: A 2020 BM vs BRF: BM 380 vs BRF 370? Let me verify BRF 2020 Serie A count+validity. If BRF 2020 had 380 valid and BM 380 → BM priority ✓ fine either way (BM correct).\n\nAlso for 2014-2019 A: BM 380 vs BRF ~369 valid → BM ✓.\n\nNow **pair trimming** for league standings/search. Implement as a method:\n\n```python\ndef _trim_league_pairs(self, matches: list[Match]) -> list[Match]:\n    pairs = defaultdict(list)\n    for m in matches: pairs[frozenset(keys)].append(m)\n    kept = []\n    for pair_matches in pairs.values():\n        if len(pair_matches) <= 2:\n            kept += pair_matches; continue\n        # prefer venue-balanced: one per side as home\n        pair_matches.sort(key=lambda m: (m.date or date.min,))\n        home_first = {}  # track\n        # greedy: pick first match where team X is home and first where team Y is home\n        # simpler: group by home team: for pair (a,b): legs with a home, legs with b home\n        a, b = ...  # need ordered pair — take from first match\n        a_home = [m for m in pair_matches if m.home_key == a]\n        b_home = [m for m in pair_matches if m.home_key == b]\n        keep = []\n        if a_home: keep.append(a_home[0])  # earliest? or latest?\n        if b_home: keep.append(b_home[0])\n        kept += keep\n    return sorted(...)\n```\n\nWhich leg to keep — earliest or latest? For mislabeled CdB 2021 matches mixed into Série B 2021: e.g. a pair meeting 3× where 2 are real league (say May & Nov) + 1 CdB (Jan/Feb or Dec)... earliest-first would keep Jan CdB + first league leg. Latest-first keeps Nov league + ... ugh. Neither is provably right. Venue-balance + earliest is a reasonable deterministic rule; document it. Hmm — actually, for the specific BRF 2021 pattern: mislabeled CdB matches cluster in Jan/Feb/Dec (early/late), while league matches run May-Nov. A smarter heuristic: keep the 2 matches closest to the season's median date? Over-engineering. \n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: is trimming even worth it? Standings for Série B 2021 would be off; that's the ONLY standings corrupted (Série B 2017: 23 teams 381 matches — pairs>2? need check; Série B 2019: 22 teams 378 — pairs?; Série B 2022: 22 teams 380!). Let me check pairs>2 for all Série B/C seasons. If several seasons have pairs>2, trimming matters more.\n\nAlso note: Série B 2015 (21 teams), 2017 (23), 2019 (22), 2022 (22) — team counts ≠ 20 suggest cross-contamination from other divisions?? Or promoted teams... A Série B season has exactly 20 teams (since 2006). 21-23 \"teams\" per season = contamination from mislabeled matches. Sigh.\n\nLet me quantify: pairs>2 per Série B season.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059335654001xDbGz60OuGq174","sessionID":"ses_fa6e4b534ffex1G5nGEhqeGwzS","messageID":"msg_059327231001HMPxqDi7vWXzWg"}}
